window.PhysicaRankPyqEm=[
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 2 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"If μ₀ and ε₀ are the permeability and permittivity of free space, respectively, then the dimension of {1;μ₀ε₀} is","o":["L²/T²","L/T²","T²/L²","T²/L"],"c":0,"s":"c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀), so 1/(μ₀ε₀) = c².\nThe dimension of c is LT⁻¹, so c² has the dimension L²T⁻² = L²/T²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 3 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"The radiation pressure exerted by a 450 W light source on a perfectly reflecting surface placed at 2 m away from it, is","o":["1.5 × 10⁻⁸ Pascals","3 × 10⁻⁸ Pascals","0","6 × 10⁻⁸ Pascals"],"c":3,"s":"Intensity at r = 2 m: I = {P;4πr²} = {450;16π} ≈ 8.95 W/m².\nFor a perfect reflector, pressure = {2I;c} = {2 × 8.95;3 × 10⁸} ≈ 6 × 10⁻⁸ Pa."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 4 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"A small mirror of mass m is suspended by a massless thread of length l. Then the small angle through which the thread will be deflected when a short pulse of laser energy E falls normal on the mirror\n(c = speed of light in vacuum and g = acceleration due to gravity)","o":["θ = {E;2mc√(gl)}","θ = {3E;4mc√(gl)}","θ = {E;mc√(gl)}","θ = {2E;mc√(gl)}"],"c":3,"s":"The mirror reflects the pulse, so the momentum given to it is 2E/c: mv = 2E/c.\nFor a small swing, ½mv² = mgl(1 − cos θ) ≈ ½mglθ², so v = θ√(gl).\nThen θ = {2E;mc√(gl)}."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 7 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"disp","st":"Displacement current in a capacitor","q":"If ε₀ denotes the permittivity of free space and φ_E is the flux of the electric field through the area bounded by a closed surface, then dimensions of ε₀{dφ_E;dt} are those of","o":["electric potential","electric charge","electric field","electric current"],"c":3,"s":"ε₀ dΦ_E/dt is the displacement current I_d, so its dimension is that of electric current."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 23 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric field of an electromagnetic wave in free space is\nE⃗ = 57 cos[7.5 × 10⁶t − 5 × 10⁻³(3x + 4y)](4î − 3ĵ) N/C\nThe associated magnetic field in Tesla is","o":["B⃗ = −{57;3 × 10⁸} cos[7.5 × 10⁶t − 5 × 10⁻³(3x + 4y)](5k̂)","B⃗ = {57;3 × 10⁸} cos[7.5 × 10⁶t − 5 × 10⁻³(3x + 4y)](5k̂)","B⃗ = −{57;3 × 10⁸} cos[7.5 × 10⁶t − 5 × 10⁻³(3x + 4y)](k̂)","B⃗ = {57;3 × 10⁸} cos[7.5 × 10⁶t − 5 × 10⁻³(3x + 4y)](k̂)"],"c":0,"s":"The phase is ωt − k⃗·r⃗ with k⃗ along (3, 4), so the wave travels along n̂ = (3î + 4ĵ)/5.\nE⃗ points along (4î − 3ĵ), whose length is 5, so the amplitude is E₀ = 57 × 5 and B₀ = E₀/c = {57 × 5;3 × 10⁸}.\nB̂ is along n̂ × Ê = (3î + 4ĵ) × (4î − 3ĵ)/25 = −k̂.\nSo B⃗ = −{57;3 × 10⁸} cos[…](5k̂)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 23 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 20 MHz travels in free space along the +x direction. At a particular point in space and time, the electric field vector of wave is E_y = 9.3 Vm⁻¹. Then, the magnetic field vector of the wave at that point is","o":["B_z = 3.1 × 10⁻⁸ T","B_z = 1.55 × 10⁻⁸ T","B_z = 9.3 × 10⁻⁸ T","B_z = 6.2 × 10⁻⁸ T"],"c":0,"s":"B = E/c = {9.3;3 × 10⁸} = 3.1 × 10⁻⁸ T.\nThe wave travels along +x and E is along y; since ŷ × ẑ = x̂, B is along +z."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 28 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"Due to presence of an EM-wave whose electric component is given by E = 100 sin(ωt − kx) NC⁻¹, a cylinder of length 200 cm holds certain amount of EM-energy inside it. If another cylinder of same length but half diameter than previous one holds same amount of EM-energy, the magnitude of the electric field of the corresponding EM wave should be modified as","o":["50 sin(ωt − kx) NC⁻¹","200 sin(ωt − kx) NC⁻¹","400 sin(ωt − kx) NC⁻¹","25 sin(ωt − kx) NC⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"Energy = (energy density) × volume = ½ε₀E₀² × {πD²;4} × L.\nHalving D makes the volume 4 times smaller. For the same energy E₀² must become 4 times larger, so E₀ doubles to 200 N/C."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 28 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The magnetic field of an E.M. wave is given by\nB⃗ = ({√3;2}î + {1;2}ĵ) 30 sin[ω(t − {z;c})] (S.I. Units).\nThe corresponding electric field in S.I. units is","o":["E⃗ = ({1;2}î + {√3;2}ĵ) 30c sin[ω(t + {z;c})]","E⃗ = ({√3;2}î − {1;2}ĵ) 30c sin[ω(t + {z;c})]","E⃗ = ({3;4}î + {1;4}ĵ) 30c cos[ω(t − {z;c})]","E⃗ = ({1;2}î − {√3;2}ĵ) 30c sin[ω(t − {z;c})]"],"c":3,"s":"The wave travels along +z (argument t − z/c), so E⃗ × B⃗ must point along +ẑ, and E = cB.\nE⃗ = c B⃗ × ẑ. With B⃗ = a î + b ĵ: (aî + bĵ) × k̂ = −a ĵ + b î.\nHere a = √3/2, b = 1/2, so E⃗ = ({1;2}î − {√3;2}ĵ) 30c sin[ω(t − z/c)]."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave propagates along +x direction in free space. The components of the electric field, E⃗ and magnetic field, B⃗ vectors associated with the wave in Cartesian frame are","o":["E_y, B_x","E_z, B_y","E_y, B_z","E_x, B_y"],"c":2,"s":"EM waves are transverse, so E and B have no component along the direction of travel (x): options (a) and (d) are out.\nE⃗ × B⃗ must point along +x. ŷ × ẑ = x̂, so E_y with B_z works; ẑ × ŷ = −x̂ does not."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 4 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric field in an electromagnetic wave is given by E⃗ = î 40 cos ω(t − {z;c}) NC⁻¹. The magnetic field induction of this wave is (in SI unit)","o":["B⃗ = k̂ {40;c} cos ω(t − {z;c})","B⃗ = î {40;c} cos ω(t − {z;c})","B⃗ = ĵ 40 cos ω(t − {z;c})","B⃗ = ĵ {40;c} cos ω(t − {z;c})"],"c":3,"s":"The wave travels along +z with E along x. Since î × ĵ = k̂, B is along +ĵ.\nB₀ = E₀/c = 40/c, so B⃗ = ĵ {40;c} cos ω(t − z/c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"disp","st":"Displacement current in a capacitor","q":"An alternating voltage of amplitude 40 V and frequency 4 kHz is applied directly across the capacitor of 12 μF. The maximum displacement current between the plates of the capacitor is nearly","o":["13 A","8 A","10 A","12 A"],"c":3,"s":"Displacement current equals the conduction current in the circuit: I_max = CωV₀.\nI_max = 12 × 10⁻⁶ × (2π × 4000) × 40 ≈ 12 A."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 6 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"Electromagnetic waves travel in a medium with speed of 1.5 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹. The relative permeability of the medium is 2.0. The relative permittivity will be","o":["2","1","5","4"],"c":0,"s":"v = c/√(μᵣεᵣ), so √(μᵣεᵣ) = {3 × 10⁸;1.5 × 10⁸} = 2 and μᵣεᵣ = 4.\nWith μᵣ = 2, εᵣ = 2."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 6 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"In the given electromagnetic wave E_y = 600 sin(ωt − kx) Vm⁻¹, intensity of the associated light beam is (in W/m²) (Given ε₀ = 9 × 10⁻¹² C²N⁻¹m⁻²)","o":["729","243","486","972"],"c":2,"s":"I = ½ε₀E₀²c = ½ × 9 × 10⁻¹² × (600)² × 3 × 10⁸ = ½ × 9 × 10⁻¹² × 3.6 × 10⁵ × 3 × 10⁸ = 486 W/m²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"Average force exerted on a non-reflecting surface at normal incidence is 2.4 × 10⁻⁴ N. If 360 W/cm² is the light energy flux during span of 1 hour 30 minutes, then the area of the surface is","o":["20 m²","0.1 m²","0.02 m²","0.2 m²"],"c":2,"s":"For an absorbing surface F = {IA;c}, so A = {Fc;I}.\nI = 360 W/cm² = 3.6 × 10⁶ W/m².\nA = {2.4 × 10⁻⁴ × 3 × 10⁸;3.6 × 10⁶} = 0.02 m². The time given is not needed."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 9 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"A plane EM wave is propagating along x direction. It has a wavelength of 4 mm. If electric field is in y direction with the maximum magnitude of 60 V m⁻¹, the equation for magnetic field is","o":["B⃗_z = 2 × 10⁻⁷ sin[{π;2} × 10³(x − 3 × 10⁸t)] k̂ T","B⃗_x = 60 sin[{π;2}(x − 3 × 10⁸t)] î T","B⃗_z = 60 sin[{π;2}(x − 3 × 10⁸t)] k̂ T","B⃗_z = 2 × 10⁻⁷ sin[{π;2}(x − 3 × 10⁸t)] k̂ T"],"c":0,"s":"B₀ = E₀/c = {60;3 × 10⁸} = 2 × 10⁻⁷ T.\nB is perpendicular to both E (y) and the travel direction (x), so it is along z (ŷ × ẑ = x̂).\nk = {2π;λ} = {2π;4 × 10⁻³} = {π;2} × 10³ m⁻¹, so the argument is {π;2} × 10³(x − ct)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 9 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"The magnetic field in a plane electromagnetic wave is B_y = (3.5 × 10⁻⁷) sin (1.5 × 10³x + 0.5 × 10¹¹t)T. The corresponding electric field will be","o":["E_y = 10.5 sin (1.5 × 10³x + 0.5 × 10¹¹t)Vm⁻¹","E_z = 1.17 sin (1.5 × 10³x + 0.5 × 10¹¹t)Vm⁻¹","E_y = 1.17 sin (1.5 × 10³x + 0.5 × 10¹¹t)Vm⁻¹","E_z = 105 sin (1.5 × 10³x + 0.5 × 10¹¹t)Vm⁻¹"],"c":3,"s":"E₀ = cB₀ = 3 × 10⁸ × 3.5 × 10⁻⁷ = 105 V/m.\nThe phase has +ωt with +kx, so the wave travels along −x. B is along y, so E must be along z (ẑ × ŷ = −x̂).\nSo E_z = 105 sin(1.5 × 10³x + 0.5 × 10¹¹t) V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"disp","st":"Displacement current in a capacitor","q":"A parallel plate capacitor has a capacitance C = 200 pF. It is connected to 230 V ac supply with an angular frequency 300 rad/s. The rms value of conduction current in the circuit and displacement current in the capacitor respectively are","o":["13.8 μA and 138 μA","14.3 μA and 143 μA","13.8 μA and 13.8 μA","1.38 μA and 1.38 μA"],"c":2,"s":"I_rms = V_rms ωC = 230 × 300 × 200 × 10⁻¹² = 1.38 × 10⁻⁵ A = 13.8 μA.\nThe displacement current in the gap equals the conduction current in the wires, so it is also 13.8 μA."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"If frequency of electromagnetic wave is 60 MHz and it travels in air along z direction then the corresponding electric and magnetic field vectors will be mutually perpendicular to each other and the wavelength of the wave (in m) is","o":["2.5","5","10","2"],"c":1,"s":"λ = {c;f} = {3 × 10⁸;60 × 10⁶} = 5 m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 27 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave propagating in x-direction is described by E_y = (200 Vm⁻¹) sin[1.5 × 10⁷t − 0.05x]. The intensity of the wave is\n(Use ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C²N⁻¹m⁻²)","o":["53.1 Wm⁻²","35.4 Wm⁻²","26.6 Wm⁻²","106.2 Wm⁻²"],"c":0,"s":"The speed is ω/k = {1.5 × 10⁷;0.05} = 3 × 10⁸ m/s = c.\nI = ½ε₀E₀²c = ½ × 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × (200)² × 3 × 10⁸ = 53.1 W/m²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 27 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"An object is placed in a medium of refractive index 3. An electromagnetic wave of intensity 6 × 10⁸ W/m² falls normally on the object and it is absorbed completely. The radiation pressure on the object would be (speed of light in free space = 3 × 10⁸ m/s)","o":["18 Nm⁻²","6 Nm⁻²","2 Nm⁻²","36 Nm⁻²"],"c":1,"s":"In a medium of index n the wave speed is v = c/n = 10⁸ m/s.\nFor complete absorption, pressure = {I;v} = {6 × 10⁸;10⁸} = 6 N/m²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 35 MHz travels in free space along the X-direction. At a particular point (in space and time) E⃗ = 9.6 ĵ V/m. The value of magnetic field at this point is","o":["9.6 × 10⁻⁸ k̂ T","3.2 × 10⁻⁸ î T","3.2 × 10⁻⁸ k̂ T","9.6 ĵ T"],"c":2,"s":"B = E/c = {9.6;3 × 10⁸} = 3.2 × 10⁻⁸ T.\nE⃗ × B⃗ must be along +x̂. Since ĵ × k̂ = î, B is along +k̂."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric field of an electromagnetic wave in free space is represented as E⃗ = E₀ cos(ωt − kz) î. The corresponding magnetic induction vector will be","o":["B⃗ = {E₀;c} cos(ωt − kz) ĵ","B⃗ = E₀c cos(ωt + kz) ĵ","B⃗ = E₀c cos(ωt − kz) ĵ","B⃗ = {E₀;c} cos(ωt + kz) ĵ"],"c":0,"s":"The wave travels along +z (ωt − kz), E is along x, and î × ĵ = k̂, so B is along +ĵ.\nB₀ = E₀/c, and B is in phase with E: B⃗ = {E₀;c} cos(ωt − kz) ĵ."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"If the total energy transferred to a surface in time t is 6.48 × 10⁵ J, then the magnitude of the total momentum delivered to this surface for complete absorption will be","o":["1.58 × 10⁻³ kg m/s","4.32 × 10⁻³ kg m/s","2.16 × 10⁻³ kg m/s","2.46 × 10⁻³ kg m/s"],"c":2,"s":"For complete absorption the momentum delivered is p = {U;c} = {6.48 × 10⁵;3 × 10⁸} = 2.16 × 10⁻³ kg m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 31 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"In a plane EM wave, the electric field oscillates sinusoidally at a frequency of 5 × 10¹⁰ Hz and an amplitude of 50 V m⁻¹. The total average energy density of the electromagnetic field of the wave is\n[Use ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C²/Nm²]","o":["2.212 × 10⁻¹⁰ J m⁻³","4.425 × 10⁻⁸ J m⁻³","1.106 × 10⁻⁸ J m⁻³","2.212 × 10⁻⁸ J m⁻³"],"c":2,"s":"Average total energy density u = ½ε₀E₀² (the electric and magnetic halves are equal).\nu = ½ × 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 50² = 1.106 × 10⁻⁸ J/m³. The frequency is not needed."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 6 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"For a plane electromagnetic wave given by E = E₀ sin(ωt − kx) and B = B₀ sin(ωt − kx), the ratio of average electric energy density to average magnetic energy density is","o":["2","4","1/2","1"],"c":3,"s":"In an EM wave the electric and magnetic energy densities are equal at every point (½ε₀E² = B²/2μ₀, because E = cB), so their averages are equal and the ratio is 1."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 6 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"The energy density associated with electric field E⃗ and magnetic field B⃗ of an electromagnetic wave in free space is given by (ε₀ - permittivity of free space, μ₀ - permeability of free space)","o":["U_E = {ε₀E²;2}, U_B = {μ₀B²;2}","U_E = {ε₀E²;2}, U_B = {B²;2μ₀}","U_E = {E²;2ε₀}, U_B = {μ₀B²;2}","U_E = {E²;2ε₀}, U_B = {B²;2μ₀}"],"c":1,"s":"The electric energy density is ½ε₀E² and the magnetic energy density is B²/2μ₀. The other forms have the wrong power of ε₀ or μ₀ (check units: ε₀E² is energy per volume)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 10 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"The energy of an electromagnetic wave contained in a small volume oscillates with","o":["zero frequency","double the frequency of the wave","half the frequency of the wave","the frequency of the wave."],"c":1,"s":"The energy density depends on E² (and B²). E = E₀ sin(ωt − kx), so E² = E₀² sin²(…) = ½E₀²(1 − cos 2(…)) oscillates at 2ω, double the frequency of the wave."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 10 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"The amplitude of magnetic field in an electromagnetic wave propagating along y-axis is 6.0 × 10⁻⁷ T. The maximum value of electric field in the electromagnetic wave is","o":["2 × 10¹⁵ V m⁻¹","5 × 10¹⁴ V m⁻¹","6.0 × 10⁻⁷ V m⁻¹","180 V m⁻¹"],"c":3,"s":"E₀ = cB₀ = 3 × 10⁸ × 6.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 180 V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 11 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"The electric field in an electromagnetic wave is given as E⃗ = 20 sin ω(t − {x;c}) ĵ NC⁻¹ where ω and c are angular frequency and velocity of electromagnetic wave respectively. The energy contained in a volume of 5 × 10⁻⁴ m³ will be\n(Given ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C²/Nm²)","o":["28.5 × 10⁻¹³ J","17.7 × 10⁻¹³ J","8.85 × 10⁻¹³ J","88.5 × 10⁻¹³ J"],"c":2,"s":"Average energy density (electric + magnetic) u = ½ε₀E₀² = ½ × 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 400 = 1.77 × 10⁻⁹ J/m³.\nEnergy = u × V = 1.77 × 10⁻⁹ × 5 × 10⁻⁴ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹³ J."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 11 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 20 MHz propagates in free space along x-direction. At a particular space and time, E⃗ = 6.6 ĵ V/m. What is B⃗ at this point?","o":["−2.2 × 10⁻⁸ î T","2.2 × 10⁻⁸ î T","2.2 × 10⁻⁸ k̂ T","−2.2 × 10⁻⁸ k̂ T"],"c":2,"s":"B = E/c = {6.6;3 × 10⁸} = 2.2 × 10⁻⁸ T.\nE⃗ × B⃗ must be along +x̂; since ĵ × k̂ = î, B is along +k̂. B has no component along the travel direction, so î is out."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 13 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"disp","st":"Maxwell’s equations and the displacement term","q":"Which of the following Maxwell’s equation is valid for time varying conditions but not valid for static conditions?","o":["∮D⃗·dA⃗ = Q","∮E⃗·dl⃗ = −{∂φ_B;∂t}","∮E⃗·dl⃗ = 0","∮B⃗·dl⃗ = μ₀I"],"c":1,"s":"For static fields ∮E⃗·dl⃗ = 0 and ∮D⃗·dA⃗ = Q still hold, and ∮B⃗·dl⃗ = μ₀I holds for steady currents.\nOnly Faraday’s law, ∮E⃗·dl⃗ = −∂φ_B/∂t, needs a changing field (for static fields the right side is zero)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 13 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"In an electromagnetic wave, at an instant and at a particular position, the electric field is along the negative z-axis and magnetic field is along the positive x-axis. Then the direction of propagation of electromagnetic wave is","o":["positive y-axis","positive z-axis","negative y-axis","at 45° angle from positive y-axis."],"c":2,"s":"The wave travels along E⃗ × B⃗. (−ẑ) × (x̂) = −(ẑ × x̂) = −ŷ, so it travels along the negative y-axis."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"The ratio of average electric energy density and total average energy density of electromagnetic wave is","o":["2","3","1/2","1"],"c":2,"s":"The electric and magnetic parts of the energy are equal, so the electric part is half of the total: ratio = ½."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 24 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"If E⃗ and K⃗ represent electric field and propagation vectors of the EM waves in vacuum, then magnetic field vector is given by (ω-angular frequency)","o":["{1;ω}(K⃗ × E⃗)","ω(K⃗ × E⃗)","K⃗ × E⃗","ω(E⃗ × K⃗)"],"c":0,"s":"B⃗ is along K̂ × Ê with magnitude E/c, where c = ω/k.\nB⃗ = {E;c}(K̂ × Ê) = {k;ω}E (K̂ × Ê) = {1;ω}(K⃗ × E⃗)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 24 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"The electric field and magnetic field components of an electromagnetic wave going through vacuum is described by\nE_x = E₀ sin(kz − ωt); B_y = B₀ sin(kz − ωt)\nthen the correct relation between E₀ and B₀ is given by","o":["E₀B₀ = ωk","E₀ = kB₀","kE₀ = ωB₀","ωE₀ = kB₀"],"c":2,"s":"E₀ = cB₀ with c = {ω;k}, so E₀ = {ω;k}B₀, that is kE₀ = ωB₀."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 25 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"An electromagnetic wave is transporting energy in the negative z direction. At a certain point and certain time the direction of electric field of the wave is along positive y direction. What will be the direction of the magnetic field of the wave at that point and instant?","o":["Positive direction of z.","Negative direction of y.","Positive direction of x.","Negative direction of x."],"c":2,"s":"E⃗ × B⃗ points along the direction of travel, −ẑ. Since ŷ × x̂ = −ẑ, B⃗ is along +x."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"A small object at rest, absorbs a light pulse of power 20 mW and duration 300 ns. Assuming speed of light as 3 × 10⁸ m/s, the momentum of the object becomes equal to","o":["2 × 10⁻¹⁷ kg m/s","1 × 10⁻¹⁷ kg m/s","3 × 10⁻¹⁷ kg m/s","0.5 × 10⁻¹⁷ kg m/s"],"c":0,"s":"Energy absorbed U = Pt = 20 × 10⁻³ × 300 × 10⁻⁹ = 6 × 10⁻⁹ J.\nMomentum p = {U;c} = {6 × 10⁻⁹;3 × 10⁸} = 2 × 10⁻¹⁷ kg m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"A point source of 100 W emits light with 5% efficiency. At a distance of 5 m from the source, the intensity produced by the electric field component is","o":["{1;40π} {W;m²}","{1;10π} {W;m²}","{1;20π} {W;m²}","{1;2π} {W;m²}"],"c":0,"s":"Light power = 5% of 100 W = 5 W. Total intensity at 5 m: I = {5;4π × 25} = {1;20π} W/m².\nThe electric and magnetic fields carry half each, so the electric part is {1;40π} W/m²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"Light wave traveling in air along x-direction is given by E_y = 540 sin π × 10⁴(x − ct) V m⁻¹. Then, the peak value of magnetic field of wave will be (Given c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","o":["18 × 10⁻⁷ T","54 × 10⁻⁷ T","54 × 10⁻⁸ T","18 × 10⁻⁸ T"],"c":0,"s":"E₀ = 540 V/m, so B₀ = {E₀;c} = {540;3 × 10⁸} = 1.8 × 10⁻⁶ T = 18 × 10⁻⁷ T."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The magnetic field of a plane electromagnetic wave is given by B⃗ = 2 × 10⁻⁸ sin(0.5 × 10³x + 1.5 × 10¹¹t) ĵ T. The amplitude of the electric field would be","o":["6 V m⁻¹ along x-axis","3 V m⁻¹ along z-axis","6 V m⁻¹ along z-axis","2 × 10⁸ V m⁻¹ along x-axis"],"c":2,"s":"E₀ = cB₀ = 3 × 10⁸ × 2 × 10⁻⁸ = 6 V/m. (Check: ω/k = {1.5 × 10¹¹;0.5 × 10³} = 3 × 10⁸ m/s.)\nThe phase is kx + ωt, so the wave travels along −x. B is along y, so E must be along z, since ẑ × ŷ = −x̂."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"The oscillating magnetic field in a plane electromagnetic wave is given by B_y = 5 × 10⁻⁶ sin1000π(5x − 4 × 10⁸t)T. The amplitude of electric field will be","o":["15 × 10² V m⁻¹","5 × 10⁻⁶ V m⁻¹","16 × 10¹² V m⁻¹","4 × 10² V m⁻¹"],"c":3,"s":"Write the phase as kx − ωt with k = 5000π and ω = 4 × 10⁸ × 1000π. The wave speed is v = ω/k = {4 × 10⁸;5} = 8 × 10⁷ m/s, which is less than c, so the wave is in a medium.\nE₀ = vB₀ = 8 × 10⁷ × 5 × 10⁻⁶ = 4 × 10² V/m. (Using c here would give the wrong value 1.5 × 10³.)"},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A beam of light travelling along X-axis is described by the electric field E_y = 900 sinω(t − {x;c}). The ratio of electric force to magnetic force on a charge q moving along Y-axis with a speed of 3 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹ will be\n(Given speed of light = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","o":["1 : 1","1 : 10","10 : 1","1 : 2"],"c":2,"s":"Electric force = qE. B is along z, so a charge moving along y is perpendicular to it and the magnetic force is qvB = qv{E;c}.\nRatio = {qE;qvE/c} = {c;v} = {3 × 10⁸;3 × 10⁷} = 10 : 1."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"Identify the correct statements from the following descriptions of various properties of electromagnetic waves.\n(A) In a plane electromagnetic wave electric field and magnetic field must be perpendicular to each other and direction of propagation of wave should be along electric field or magnetic field.\n(B) The energy in electromagnetic wave is divided equally between electric and magnetic fields.\n(C) Both electric field and magnetic field are parallel to each other and perpendicular to the direction of propagation of wave.\n(D) The electric field, magnetic field and direction of propagation of wave must be perpendicular to each other.\n(E) The ratio of amplitude of magnetic field to the amplitude of electric field is equal to speed of light.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","o":["(D) only","(B) and (D) only","(B), (C) and (E) only","(A), (B) and (E) only"],"c":1,"s":"(A) is false: the wave travels perpendicular to E and B, not along them.\n(B) is true: the electric and magnetic energy densities are equal.\n(C) is false: E and B are perpendicular to each other.\n(D) is true: E, B and the direction of travel are mutually perpendicular.\n(E) is false: E₀/B₀ = c, so B₀/E₀ = 1/c.\nOnly (B) and (D) are correct."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"An electromagnetic wave travels from air, through medium 1 (μᵣ = 1, εᵣ = 4), into medium 2 (μᵣ = 1, εᵣ = 9), as in the figure of the paper (Air | Medium 1 | Medium 2). The speed of light v₂ in medium 2 will be\n(Given c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","o":["1.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹","0.5 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹","1.5 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹","3.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹"],"c":0,"s":"The speed in a medium depends only on that medium: v₂ = {c;√(μᵣεᵣ)} = {3 × 10⁸;√9} = 1.0 × 10⁸ m/s. Medium 1 does not matter."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"Sunlight falls normally on a surface of area 36 cm² and exerts an average force of 7.2 × 10⁻⁹ N within a time period of 20 minutes. Considering a case of complete absorption, the energy flux of incident light is","o":["25.92 × 10² W/cm²","8.64 × 10⁻⁶ W/cm²","6.0 W/cm²","0.06 W/cm²"],"c":3,"s":"For complete absorption F = {IA;c}, so I = {Fc;A}.\nI = {7.2 × 10⁻⁹ × 3 × 10⁸;36 × 10⁻⁴} = 600 W/m² = 0.06 W/cm². The 20 minutes is not needed."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 24 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave travels in a medium of relative permeability 1.61 and relative permittivity 6.44. If magnitude of magnetic intensity is 4.5 × 10⁻² A m⁻¹ at a point, what will be the approximate magnitude of electric field intensity at that point?\n(Given : Permeability of free space, μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ NA⁻², speed of light in vacuum c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","o":["16.96 V m⁻¹","2.25 × 10⁻² V m⁻¹","8.48 V m⁻¹","6.75 × 10⁶ V m⁻¹"],"c":2,"s":"In the medium E = vB with B = μ₀μᵣH and v = {c;√(μᵣεᵣ)}.\nE = {c;√(μᵣεᵣ)} μ₀μᵣH = μ₀cH √{μᵣ;εᵣ} = (4π × 10⁻⁷ × 3 × 10⁸)(4.5 × 10⁻²)√{1.61;6.44}.\n√{1.61;6.44} = 0.5, so E ≈ 377 × 4.5 × 10⁻² × 0.5 ≈ 8.48 V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 24 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"An electric bulb is rated as 200 W. What will be the peak magnetic field at 4 m distance produced by the radiations coming from this bulb? Consider this bulb as a point source with 3.5% efficiency.","o":["1.19 × 10⁻⁸ T","1.71 × 10⁻⁸ T","0.84 × 10⁻⁸ T","3.36 × 10⁻⁸ T"],"c":1,"s":"Light power = 0.035 × 200 = 7 W. At r = 4 m: I = {7;4π × 16} ≈ 0.0348 W/m².\nI = ½ε₀E₀²c gives E₀ = √{2I;ε₀c} = √{0.0696;8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 3 × 10⁸} ≈ 5.1 V/m.\nB₀ = {E₀;c} ≈ 1.71 × 10⁻⁸ T."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"The electric field in an electromagnetic wave is given by E = 56.5 sinω(t − x/c) N C⁻¹. Find the intensity of the wave if it is propagating along x-axis in the free space.\n(Given ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C² N⁻¹ m⁻²)","o":["5.65 W m⁻²","4.24 W m⁻²","1.9 × 10⁻⁷ W m⁻²","56.5 W m⁻²"],"c":1,"s":"I = ½ε₀E₀²c = ½ × 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × (56.5)² × 3 × 10⁸ ≈ 4.24 W/m²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"The electromagnetic waves travel in a medium at a speed of 2.0 × 10⁸ m/s. The relative permeability of the medium is 1.0. The relative permittivity of the medium will be","o":["2.25","4.25","6.25","8.25"],"c":0,"s":"v = {c;√(μᵣεᵣ)} gives √(μᵣεᵣ) = {3 × 10⁸;2 × 10⁸} = 1.5.\nWith μᵣ = 1, εᵣ = 1.5² = 2.25."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"If electric field intensity of a uniform plane electromagnetic wave is given as\nE⃗ = −301.6 sin(kz − ωt) â_x + 452.4 sin(kz − ωt) â_y {V;m}.\nThen, magnetic intensity ’H⃗’ of this wave in A m⁻¹ will be\n[Given : Speed of light in vacuum c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, Permeability of vacuum μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ NA⁻²]","o":["+0.8 sin(kz − ωt) â_y + 0.8 sin(kz − ωt) â_x","+1.0 × 10⁻⁶ sin(kz − ωt) â_y + 1.5 × 10⁻⁶ (kz − ωt) â_x","−0.8 sin(kz − ωt) â_y − 1.2 sin(kz − ωt) â_x","−1.0 × 10⁻⁶ sin(kz − ωt) â_y − 1.5 × 10⁻⁶ sin(kz − ωt) â_x"],"c":2,"s":"The wave travels along +z. B⃗ = {1;c}(ẑ × E⃗), and H⃗ = B⃗/μ₀.\nẑ × â_x = â_y and ẑ × â_y = −â_x, so ẑ × E⃗ = −301.6 â_y − 452.4 â_x.\nμ₀c = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 3 × 10⁸ ≈ 377, so H⃗ = −{301.6;377} â_y − {452.4;377} â_x = −0.8 sin(kz − ωt) â_y − 1.2 sin(kz − ωt) â_x."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"A radar sends an electromagnetic signal of electric field (E₀) = 2.25 V/m and magnetic field (B₀) = 1.5 × 10⁻⁸ T which strikes a target on line of sight at a distance of 3 km in a medium. After that, a part of signal (echo) reflects back towards the radar with same velocity and by same path. If the signal was transmitted at time t = 0 from radar, then after how much time echo will reach to the radar?","o":["2.0 × 10⁻⁵ s","4.0 × 10⁻⁵ s","1.0 × 10⁻⁵ s","8.0 × 10⁻⁵ s"],"c":1,"s":"Speed of the wave in the medium: v = {E₀;B₀} = {2.25;1.5 × 10⁻⁸} = 1.5 × 10⁸ m/s.\nThe echo travels 3 km there and 3 km back: t = {6000;1.5 × 10⁸} = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"An EM wave propagating in x-direction has a wavelength of 8 mm. The electric field vibrating y-direction has maximum magnitude of 60 Vm⁻¹. Choose the correct equations for electric and magnetic fields if the EM wave is propagating in vacuum.","o":["E⃗_y = 60 sin[{π;4} × 10³(x − 3 × 10⁸t)] ĵ V m⁻¹\nB⃗_z = 2 sin[{π;4} × 10³(x − 3 × 10⁸t)] k̂ T","E⃗_y = 60 sin[{π;4} × 10³(x − 3 × 10⁸t)] ĵ V m⁻¹\nB⃗_z = 2 × 10⁻⁷ sin[{π;4} × 10³(x − 3 × 10⁸t)] k̂ T","E⃗_y = 2 × 10⁻⁷ sin[{π;4} × 10³(x − 3 × 10⁸t)] ĵ V m⁻¹\nB⃗_z = 60 sin[{π;4} × 10³(x − 3 × 10⁸t)] k̂ T","E⃗_y = 2 × 10⁻⁷ sin[{π;4} × 10⁴(x − 4 × 10⁸t)] ĵ V m⁻¹\nB⃗_z = 60 sin[{π;4} × 10⁴(x − 4 × 10⁸t)] k̂ T"],"c":1,"s":"k = {2π;λ} = {2π;8 × 10⁻³} = {π;4} × 10³ m⁻¹, and the wave speed in vacuum is c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s, so the argument is {π;4} × 10³(x − 3 × 10⁸t).\nB₀ = {E₀;c} = {60;3 × 10⁸} = 2 × 10⁻⁷ T, along z (ŷ × ẑ = x̂).\nOnly option (b) has all three correct."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 1 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"Electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave propagating through a non-magnetic medium is given by E = 20cos(2 × 10¹⁰t − 200x) V/m. The dielectric constant of the medium is equal to\n(Take μᵣ = 1)","o":["2","{1;3}","9","3"],"c":2,"s":"Wave speed v = {ω;k} = {2 × 10¹⁰;200} = 10⁸ m/s.\nn = {c;v} = 3 and n = √(μᵣεᵣ) = √εᵣ, so the dielectric constant εᵣ = 9."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A light beam is described by E = 800 sinω(t − {x;c}). An electron is allowed to move normal to the propagation of light beam with a speed of 3 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹. What is the maximum magnetic force exerted on the electron?","o":["1.28 × 10⁻²¹ N","12.8 × 10⁻¹⁸ N","1.28 × 10⁻¹⁸ N","12.8 × 10⁻¹⁷ N"],"c":1,"s":"B₀ = {E₀;c} = {800;3 × 10⁸} = 2.67 × 10⁻⁶ T.\nThe electron moves normal to the beam, so v is perpendicular to B (B is perpendicular to the beam): F = evB₀ = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 3 × 10⁷ × 2.67 × 10⁻⁶ ≈ 1.28 × 10⁻¹⁷ N = 12.8 × 10⁻¹⁸ N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"Electric field in a plane electromagnetic wave is given by\nE = 50 sin(500x − 10 × 10¹⁰t) V/m\nThe velocity of electromagnetic wave in this medium is\n(Given c = speed of light in vacuum)","o":["{3;2}c","c","{c;2}","{2;3}c"],"c":3,"s":"v = {ω;k} = {10 × 10¹⁰;500} = 2 × 10⁸ m/s = {2;3}c."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 20 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"disp","st":"Displacement current in a capacitor","q":"AC voltage V(t) = 20 sinωt of frequency 50 Hz is applied to a parallel plate capacitor. The separation between the plates is 2 mm and the area is 1 m². The amplitude of the oscillating displacement current for the applied AC voltage is.\n[Take ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² F m⁻¹]","o":["27.29 μA","83.37 μA","55.58 μA","21.14 μA"],"c":0,"s":"C = {ε₀A;d} = {8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 1;2 × 10⁻³} = 4.43 × 10⁻⁹ F.\nThe displacement current in the gap equals the circuit current: I₀ = ωCV₀ = (2π × 50)(4.43 × 10⁻⁹)(20) ≈ 2.8 × 10⁻⁵ A ≈ 28 μA.\nOf the options, 27.29 μA is the only one near this value."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 20 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"In an electromagnetic wave the electric field vector and magnetic field vector are given as E⃗ = E₀î and B⃗ = B₀k̂ respectively. The direction of propagation of electromagnetic wave is along","o":["k̂","(−ĵ)","(−k̂)","ĵ"],"c":1,"s":"The wave travels along E⃗ × B⃗. î × k̂ = −ĵ, so it travels along −ĵ."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 22 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"Intensity of sunlight is observed as 0.092 Wm⁻² at a point in free space. What will be the peak value of magnetic field at that point?\n(ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C²N⁻¹m⁻²)","o":["1.96 × 10⁻⁸ T","2.77 × 10⁻⁸ T","5.88 T","8.31 T"],"c":1,"s":"I = ½ε₀E₀²c, so E₀ = √{2I;ε₀c} = √{0.184;8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 3 × 10⁸} ≈ 8.3 V/m.\nB₀ = {E₀;c} = {8.3;3 × 10⁸} ≈ 2.77 × 10⁻⁸ T."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 25 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A linearly polarized electromagnetic wave in vacuum is E⃗ = 3.1cos[(1.8)z − (5.4 × 10⁶)t] î N/C is incident normally on a perfectly reflecting wall at z = a. Choose the correct option.","o":["The reflected wave will be 3.1cos[(1.8)z + (5.4 × 10⁶)t] î N/C.","The wavelength is 5.4 m.","The transmitted wave will be 3.1cos[(1.8)z − (5.4 × 10⁶)t] î N/C.","The frequency of electromagnetic wave is 54 × 10⁴ Hz."],"c":0,"s":"From the argument, k = 1.8 m⁻¹ and ω = 5.4 × 10⁶ rad/s.\n(b) λ = 2π/k = 3.5 m, not 5.4 m: false. (d) f = ω/2π ≈ 8.6 × 10⁵ Hz, not 54 × 10⁴ Hz: false. (c) A perfect reflector transmits nothing: false.\n(a) The reflected wave has the same amplitude and frequency and travels along −z (ωt together with +kz). That is the only statement that fits (its constant phase depends on the wall position a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"The relative permittivity of distilled water is 81. The velocity of light in it will be (Given μᵣ = 1)","o":["4.33 × 10⁷ m/s","2.33 × 10⁷ m/s","5.33 × 10⁷ m/s","3.33 × 10⁷ m/s"],"c":3,"s":"v = {c;√(μᵣεᵣ)} = {3 × 10⁸;√81} = {3 × 10⁸;9} = 3.33 × 10⁷ m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 1 (also JEE Main 2019 · 9 Jan · Shift 2)","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"For an electromagnetic wave travelling in free space, the relation between average energy densities due to electric (U_e) and magnetic (U_m) fields is","o":["U_e < U_m","U_e ≠ U_m","U_e > U_m","U_e = U_m"],"c":3,"s":"Since E = cB, the electric energy density ½ε₀E² equals the magnetic energy density B²/2μ₀ (using c² = 1/μ₀ε₀). So U_e = U_m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 500 MHz is travelling in vacuum along y-direction. At a particular point in space and time, B⃗ = 8.0 × 10⁻⁸ ẑ T. The value of electric field at this point is (speed of light = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹) x̂, ŷ, ẑ are unit vectors along x, y and z directions.","o":["−2.6 ŷ V m⁻¹","24 x̂ V m⁻¹","2.6 x̂ V m⁻¹","−24 x̂ V m⁻¹"],"c":3,"s":"E = cB = 3 × 10⁸ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁸ = 24 V/m, perpendicular to both B (z) and the travel direction (y), so along ±x.\nE⃗ × B⃗ must be along +ŷ. (−x̂) × ẑ = −(x̂ × ẑ) = +ŷ, so E⃗ = −24 x̂ V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 18 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 100 MHz is travelling in vacuum along the x-direction. At a particular point in space and time, B⃗ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁸ k̂ T. (where, k̂ is unit vector along z-direction) What is E⃗ at this point?\n(Speed of light = 3 × 10⁸ m/s)","o":["6.0 ĵ V/m","0.6 k̂ V/m","6.0 k̂ V/m","0.6 ĵ V/m"],"c":0,"s":"E = cB = 3 × 10⁸ × 2.0 × 10⁻⁸ = 6.0 V/m, perpendicular to B (z) and to the travel direction (x), so along ±y.\nE⃗ × B⃗ must be along +x̂, and ŷ × ẑ = x̂, so E⃗ = +6.0 ĵ V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 18 Mar · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave propagating along y-direction can have the following pair of electric field (E⃗) and magnetic field (B⃗) components.","o":["E_x, B_y or E_y, B_x","E_x, B_y or E_z, B_z","E_x, B_z or E_z, B_x","E_y, B_x or E_x, B_y"],"c":2,"s":"The wave travels along y, so E and B lie in the x–z plane (no y component): E_x or E_z, and B_x or B_z.\nE⃗ and B⃗ must also be perpendicular to each other, so the pair is (E_x, B_z) or (E_z, B_x) (then E⃗ × B⃗ is along ∓ŷ, giving the direction by the sign)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 2 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave, has frequency of 2.0 × 10¹⁰ Hz and its energy density is 1.02 × 10⁻⁸ J/m³ in vacuum. The amplitude of the magnetic field of the wave is close to ({1;4πε₀} = 9 × 10⁹ {N m²;C²} and speed of light = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","o":["150 nT","160 nT","180 nT","190 nT"],"c":1,"s":"The electric and magnetic parts of the average energy density are equal, so the total is u = 2 × {B₀²;4μ₀} = {B₀²;2μ₀}.\nμ₀ = {1;ε₀c²} = 4π × 9 × 10⁹ / (9 × 10¹⁶) = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T m/A.\nB₀ = √(2μ₀u) = √(2 × 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 1.02 × 10⁻⁸) ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁷ T = 160 nT."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 2 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"In a plane electromagnetic wave, the directions of electric field and magnetic field are represented by k̂ and 2î − 2ĵ, respectively. What is the unit vector along direction of propagation of the wave?","o":["{1;√5}(î + 2ĵ)","{1;√2}(ĵ + k̂)","{1;√2}(î + ĵ)","{1;√5}(2î + ĵ)"],"c":2,"s":"The wave travels along E⃗ × B⃗ = k̂ × (2î − 2ĵ) = 2(k̂ × î) − 2(k̂ × ĵ) = 2ĵ + 2î.\nThe unit vector is (2î + 2ĵ)/√8 = {1;√2}(î + ĵ)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 3 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The magnetic field of a plane electromagnetic wave is B⃗ = 3 × 10⁻⁸ sin[200π(y + ct)] î T, where c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ is the speed of light. The corresponding electric field is","o":["E⃗ = 9 sin[200π(y + ct)] k̂ V/m","E⃗ = −10⁻⁶ sin[200π(y + ct)] k̂ V/m","E⃗ = 3 × 10⁻⁸ sin[200π(y + ct)] k̂ V/m","E⃗ = −9 sin[200π(y + ct)] k̂ V/m."],"c":3,"s":"E₀ = cB₀ = 3 × 10⁸ × 3 × 10⁻⁸ = 9 V/m.\nThe argument is y + ct, so the wave travels along −y. E⃗ × B⃗ must be along −ŷ with B along î. Since k̂ × î = +ĵ, E must point along −k̂.\nE⃗ = −9 sin[200π(y + ct)] k̂ V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 3 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave propagating along the x direction in vacuum is E⃗ = E₀ ĵ cos(ωt − kx). The magnetic field B⃗, at the moment t = 0 is","o":["B⃗ = {E₀;√(μ₀ε₀)} cos(kx) k̂","B⃗ = E₀√(μ₀ε₀) cos(kx) ĵ","B⃗ = E₀√(μ₀ε₀) cos(kx) k̂","B⃗ = {E₀;√(μ₀ε₀)} cos(kx) ĵ"],"c":2,"s":"B is perpendicular to E (y) and to the travel direction (x); ĵ × k̂ = î, so B is along +k̂.\nB₀ = {E₀;c} = E₀√(μ₀ε₀), and B is in phase with E, so at t = 0 B⃗ = E₀√(μ₀ε₀) cos(kx) k̂."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 4 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave is given by E⃗ = E₀(x̂ + ŷ) sin(kz − ωt). Its magnetic field will be given by","o":["{E₀;c}(x̂ − ŷ) cos(kz − ωt)","{E₀;c}(x̂ − ŷ) sin(kz − ωt)","{E₀;c}(−x̂ + ŷ) sin(kz − ωt)","{E₀;c}(x̂ + ŷ) sin(kz − ωt)"],"c":2,"s":"The wave travels along +z and B is in phase with E (sin, not cos), with B⃗ = {1;c}(ẑ × E⃗).\nẑ × (x̂ + ŷ) = ŷ − x̂, so B⃗ = {E₀;c}(−x̂ + ŷ) sin(kz − ωt)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 5 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"An electron is constrained to move along the y-axis with a speed of 0.1 c (c is the speed of light) in the presence of electromagnetic wave, whose electric field is E⃗ = 30 ĵ sin(1.5 × 10⁷t − 5 × 10⁻²x) V/m. The maximum magnetic force experienced by the electron will be (given c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and electron charge = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)","o":["3.2 × 10⁻¹⁸ N","2.4 × 10⁻¹⁸ N","4.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ N","1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ N"],"c":2,"s":"E₀ = 30 V/m, so B₀ = {E₀;c} = {30;3 × 10⁸} = 10⁻⁷ T, along z (the wave travels along x, E along y).\nThe electron moves along y, perpendicular to B: F = evB₀ = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × (0.1 × 3 × 10⁸) × 10⁻⁷ = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 6 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"For a plane electromagnetic wave, the magnetic field at a point x and time t is\nB⃗(x, t) = [1.2 × 10⁻⁷ sin(0.5 × 10³x + 1.5 × 10¹¹t) k̂] T.\nThe instantaneous electric field E⃗ corresponding to B⃗ is (speed of light c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","o":["E⃗(x, t) = [−36 sin(0.5 × 10³x + 1.5 × 10¹¹t) ĵ] {V;m}","E⃗(x, t) = [36 sin(1 × 10³x + 0.5 × 10¹¹t) ĵ] {V;m}","E⃗(x, t) = [36 sin(0.5 × 10³x + 1.5 × 10¹¹t) k̂] {V;m}","E⃗(x, t) = [36 sin(1 × 10³x + 1.5 × 10¹¹t) î] {V;m}"],"c":0,"s":"E₀ = cB₀ = 3 × 10⁸ × 1.2 × 10⁻⁷ = 36 V/m, and E has the same argument as B (so (b) and (d) are out).\nThe phase is kx + ωt, so the wave travels along −x. E is perpendicular to B (z) and to x, so it is along ±y; ĵ × k̂ = î, so (−ĵ) × k̂ = −î is the required direction.\nE⃗ = −36 sin(0.5 × 10³x + 1.5 × 10¹¹t) ĵ V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 7 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"If the magnetic field in a plane electromagnetic wave is given by B⃗ = 3 × 10⁻⁸ sin(1.6 × 10³x + 48 × 10¹⁰t) ĵ T, then what will be the expression for electric field?","o":["E⃗ = (9 sin(1.6 × 10³x + 48 × 10¹⁰t) k̂ V m⁻¹)","E⃗ = (60 sin(1.6 × 10³x + 48 × 10¹⁰t) k̂ V m⁻¹)","E⃗ = (3 × 10⁻⁸ sin(1.6 × 10³x + 48 × 10¹⁰t) î V m⁻¹)","E⃗ = (3 × 10⁻⁸ sin(1.6 × 10³x + 48 × 10¹⁰t) ĵ V m⁻¹)"],"c":0,"s":"ω/k = {48 × 10¹⁰;1.6 × 10³} = 3 × 10⁸ m/s = c, so E₀ = cB₀ = 9 V/m (options (c), (d) have the wrong size and direction).\nThe wave travels along −x and B is along y; k̂ × ĵ = −î, so E is along +k̂.\nE⃗ = 9 sin(1.6 × 10³x + 48 × 10¹⁰t) k̂ V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 7 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave is given by\nE⃗ = E₀ {î + ĵ;√2} cos(kz + ωt).\nAt t = 0, a positively charged particle is at the point (x, y, z) = (0, 0, {π;k}). If its instantaneous velocity at (t = 0) is v₀k̂, the force acting on it due to the wave is","o":["antiparallel to {î + ĵ;√2}","parallel to k̂","zero","parallel to {î + ĵ;√2}"],"c":0,"s":"At t = 0, z = π/k: cos(kz) = cos π = −1, so E⃗ = −E₀{î + ĵ;√2}: the electric force on the positive charge is along −(î + ĵ)/√2.\nThe wave travels along −z, so B⃗ = {1;c}(−ẑ × E⃗) = −{E₀;c}{ĵ − î;√2} cos(kz + ωt) = +{E₀;c}{ĵ − î;√2} at this instant.\nMagnetic force qv⃗ × B⃗ = q v₀{E₀;c} k̂ × {ĵ − î;√2} = −q{v₀;c}E₀ {î + ĵ;√2}: also along −(î + ĵ)/√2.\nBoth forces are antiparallel to (î + ĵ)/√2."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 8 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 25 GHz is propagating in vacuum along the z-direction. At a particular point in space and time, the magnetic field is given by B⃗ = 5 × 10⁻⁸ ĵ T. The corresponding electric field E⃗ is (speed of light c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","o":["−1.66 × 10⁻¹⁶ î V/m","1.66 × 10⁻¹⁶ î V/m","−15 î V/m","15 î V/m"],"c":3,"s":"E = cB = 3 × 10⁸ × 5 × 10⁻⁸ = 15 V/m.\nE⃗ × B⃗ must point along +ẑ; since î × ĵ = k̂, E⃗ = +15 î V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 9 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric fields of two plane electromagnetic plane waves in vacuum are given by\nE⃗₁ = E₀ĵ cos(ωt − kx) and E⃗₂ = E₀k̂ cos(ωt − ky)\nAt t = 0, a particle of charge q is at origin with a velocity v⃗ = 0.8cĵ (c is the speed of light in vacuum). The instantaneous force experienced by the particle is","o":["E₀q(0.8î − ĵ + 0.4k̂)","E₀q(0.4î − 3ĵ + 0.8k̂)","E₀q(−0.8î + ĵ + k̂)","E₀q(0.8î + ĵ + 0.2k̂)"],"c":3,"s":"At the origin, t = 0: E⃗₁ = E₀ĵ, E⃗₂ = E₀k̂.\nWave 1 travels along +x, so B⃗₁ = {1;c}(x̂ × E⃗₁) = {E₀;c}k̂. Wave 2 travels along +y, so B⃗₂ = {1;c}(ŷ × E⃗₂) = {E₀;c}î.\nMagnetic part: qv⃗ × B⃗ = q(0.8c)ĵ × {E₀;c}(k̂ + î) = 0.8qE₀(î − k̂).\nF⃗ = qE₀(ĵ + k̂) + 0.8qE₀(î − k̂) = E₀q(0.8î + ĵ + 0.2k̂)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 9 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave is propagating along the direction {î + ĵ;√2}, with its polarization along the direction k̂. The correct form of the magnetic field of the wave would be (Here B₀ is an appropriate constant.)","o":["B₀ {î + ĵ;√2} cos(ωt − k{î + ĵ;√2})","B₀ k̂ cos(ωt − k{î + ĵ;√2})","B₀ {î − ĵ;√2} cos(ωt − k{î + ĵ;√2})","B₀ {ĵ − î;√2} cos(ωt + k{î + ĵ;√2})"],"c":2,"s":"B⃗ is along n̂ × Ê, where n̂ = (î + ĵ)/√2 and Ê = k̂.\n(î + ĵ) × k̂ = −ĵ + î, so B̂ = (î − ĵ)/√2.\nThe wave travels along +n̂, so the phase is ωt − k⃗·r⃗ (a minus sign), which rules out (d); (a) is parallel to the travel direction and (b) is parallel to E."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave travels in free space along the x-direction. The electric field component of the wave at a particular point of space and time is E = 6 V m⁻¹ along y-direction. Its corresponding magnetic field component, B would be","o":["6 × 10⁻⁸ T along x-direction","2 × 10⁻⁸ T along y-direction","2 × 10⁻⁸ T along z-direction","6 × 10⁻⁸ T along z-direction"],"c":2,"s":"B = {E;c} = {6;3 × 10⁸} = 2 × 10⁻⁸ T.\nB is perpendicular to both the travel direction (x) and E (y), so it is along z."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The magnetic field of an electromagnetic wave is given by\nB⃗ = 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ cos(2 × 10⁷z + 6 × 10¹⁵t)(2î + ĵ) {Wb;m²}\nThe associated electric field will be","o":["E⃗ = 4.8 × 10² cos(2 × 10⁷z + 6 × 10¹⁵t)(−î + 2ĵ) V/m","E⃗ = 4.8 × 10² cos(2 × 10⁷z − 6 × 10¹⁵t)(−2ĵ + î) V/m","E⃗ = 4.8 × 10² cos(2 × 10⁷z + 6 × 10¹⁵t)(î − 2ĵ) V/m","E⃗ = 4.8 × 10² cos(2 × 10⁷z − 6 × 10¹⁵t)(2î + ĵ) V/m"],"c":0,"s":"The phase has +ωt with +kz, so the wave travels along −z; this rules out (b) and (d). E must be perpendicular to B⃗ ∝ (2î + ĵ), so E⃗ ∝ ±(−î + 2ĵ).\nTake E⃗ = a(−î + 2ĵ): E⃗ × B⃗ = a(−î + 2ĵ) × b(2î + ĵ) = ab(−1 − 4)k̂ = −5ab k̂, which is along −ẑ for a, b > 0. So the sign is +, i.e. (−î + 2ĵ), not (î − 2ĵ).\nSize: E₀ = cB₀ = 3 × 10⁸ × 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ = 4.8 × 10² per unit-length vector."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 9 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The magnetic field of a plane electromagnetic wave is given by B⃗ = B₀î[cos(kz − ωt)] + B₁ĵ cos(kz + ωt) where B₀ = 3 × 10⁻⁵ T and B₁ = 2 × 10⁻⁶ T. The rms value of the force experienced by a stationary charge Q = 10⁻⁴ C at z = 0 is closest to","o":["0.1 N","3 × 10⁻² N","0.9 N","0.6 N"],"c":3,"s":"A stationary charge feels only the electric force F = QE.\nThe two parts are waves with amplitudes E₁ = cB₀ = 9 × 10³ V/m (travelling along +z, E along −ĵ) and E₂ = cB₁ = 600 V/m (travelling along −z, E along −î). At z = 0 both vary as cos ωt, in phase and perpendicular, so the amplitude is √(9000² + 600²) ≈ 9.02 × 10³ V/m.\nPeak force = 10⁻⁴ × 9.02 × 10³ ≈ 0.90 N; rms = peak/√2 ≈ 0.64 N, closest to 0.6 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 9 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"50 W/m² energy density of sunlight is normally incident on the surface of a solar panel. Some part of incident energy (25%) is reflected from the surface and the rest is absorbed. The force exerted on 1 m² surface area will be close to (c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s)","o":["20 × 10⁻⁸ N","10 × 10⁻⁸ N","35 × 10⁻⁸ N","15 × 10⁻⁸ N"],"c":0,"s":"Treat 50 W/m² as the intensity I. Per unit area the force is {I;c} for the absorbed part (75%) and {2I;c} for the reflected part (25%).\nF = {I;c}(0.75 + 2 × 0.25)A = {50;3 × 10⁸} × 1.25 × 1 ≈ 2.1 × 10⁻⁷ N, close to 20 × 10⁻⁸ N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 10 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave is E⃗ = E₀î cos(kz) cos(ωt). The corresponding magnetic field B⃗ is then given by","o":["B⃗ = {E₀;c} ĵ sin(kz) sin(ωt)","B⃗ = {E₀;c} k̂ sin(kz) cos(ωt)","B⃗ = {E₀;c} ĵ cos(kz) sin(ωt)","B⃗ = {E₀;c} ĵ sin(kz) cos(ωt)"],"c":0,"s":"Use Faraday’s law, ∇ × E⃗ = −∂B⃗/∂t. For E⃗ = E_x(z, t) î: (∇ × E⃗)_y = ∂E_x/∂z = −kE₀ sin(kz) cos(ωt).\nSo ∂B_y/∂t = kE₀ sin(kz) cos(ωt), which integrates to B_y = {kE₀;ω} sin(kz) sin(ωt) = {E₀;c} sin(kz) sin(ωt).\nB is along ĵ (so (b) is out) and it is a standing wave with space and time parts exchanged, which rules out (c) and (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 10 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"Light is incident normally on a completely absorbing surface with an energy flux of 25 W cm⁻². If the surface has an area of 25 cm², the momentum transferred to the surface in 40 min time duration will be","o":["1.4 × 10⁻⁶ N s","5.0 × 10⁻³ N s","6.3 × 10⁻⁴ N s","3.5 × 10⁻⁶ N s"],"c":1,"s":"Energy absorbed U = (flux)(area)(time) = 25 × 25 × (40 × 60) = 1.5 × 10⁶ J.\nFor complete absorption p = {U;c} = {1.5 × 10⁶;3 × 10⁸} = 5.0 × 10⁻³ N s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"An electromagnetic wave is represented by the electric field E⃗ = E₀ n̂ sin[ωt + (6y − 8z)]. Taking unit vectors in x, y and z directions to be î, ĵ, k̂, the direction of propagation ŝ, is","o":["ŝ = ({−3ĵ + 4k̂;5})","ŝ = ({−4k̂ + 3ĵ;5})","ŝ = ({4ĵ − 3k̂;5})","ŝ = ({3î − 4ĵ;5})"],"c":0,"s":"The phase is ωt + k⃗·r⃗ with k⃗ ∝ (6ĵ − 8k̂). With a plus sign between ωt and k⃗·r⃗ the wave travels along −k⃗.\nSo ŝ = −{6ĵ − 8k̂;10} = {−3ĵ + 4k̂;5}."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave having a frequency ν = 23.9 GHz propagates along the positive z-direction in free space. The peak value of the electric field is 60 V/m. Which among the following is the acceptable magnetic field component in the electromagnetic wave?","o":["B⃗ = 2 × 10⁻⁷ sin(0.5 × 10³z − 1.5 × 10¹¹t) î","B⃗ = 60 sin(0.5 × 10³x + 1.5 × 10¹¹t) k̂","B⃗ = 2 × 10⁷ sin(0.5 × 10³z + 1.5 × 10¹¹t) î","B⃗ = 2 × 10⁻⁷ sin(1.5 × 10²x + 0.5 × 10¹¹t) ĵ"],"c":0,"s":"B₀ = {E₀;c} = {60;3 × 10⁸} = 2 × 10⁻⁷ T. ω = 2πν ≈ 1.5 × 10¹¹ rad/s and k = {ω;c} = 0.5 × 10³ m⁻¹.\nThe wave travels along +z, so the phase is kz − ωt and B is along x or y: only (a) has all of these.\n(b) has a field of 60 and a k̂ component along the travel direction; (c) has the wrong size and sign; (d) travels along x with the wrong k and ω."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 9 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 50 MHz travels in free space along the positive x-direction. At a particular point in space and time, E⃗ = 6.3 ĵ V/m. The corresponding magnetic field B⃗, at that point will be","o":["6.3 × 10⁻⁸ k̂ T","18.9 × 10⁸ k̂ T","18.9 × 10⁻⁸ k̂ T","2.1 × 10⁻⁸ k̂ T"],"c":3,"s":"B = {E;c} = {6.3;3 × 10⁸} = 2.1 × 10⁻⁸ T.\nĵ × k̂ = î, so B is along +k̂."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 10 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"If the magnetic field of a plane electromagnetic wave is given by (The speed of light = 3 × 10⁸ m/s)\nB = 100 × 10⁻⁶ sin[2π × 2 × 10¹⁵(t − {x;c})]\nthen the maximum electric field associated with it is","o":["4.5 × 10⁴ N/C","4 × 10⁴ N/C","6 × 10⁴ N/C","3 × 10⁴ N/C"],"c":3,"s":"E₀ = cB₀ = 3 × 10⁸ × 100 × 10⁻⁶ = 3 × 10⁴ N/C. The frequency is not needed."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 10 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric field of a plane polarized electromagnetic wave in free space at time t = 0 is given by an expression\nE⃗(x, y) = 10ĵ cos[(6x + 8z)].\nThe magnetic field B⃗(x, z, t) is given by (c is the velocity of light)","o":["{1;c}(6k̂ − 8î) cos[(6x + 8z − 10ct)]","{1;c}(6k̂ + 8î) cos[(6x + 8z − 10ct)]","{1;c}(6k̂ − 8î) cos[(6x + 8z + 10ct)]","{1;c}(6k̂ + 8î) cos[(6x − 8z + 10ct)]"],"c":0,"s":"The wave vector is k⃗ = 6î + 8k̂, so k = 10 m⁻¹ and ω = kc = 10c; the travel direction is n̂ = (3î + 4k̂)/5. At t = 0 the phase is k⃗·r⃗ = 6x + 8z, so the phase is 6x + 8z − 10ct (the wave travels along +n̂; options (c), (d) have a +ωt).\nB⃗ = {E₀;c}(n̂ × ĵ) with E₀ = 10: n̂ × ĵ = {3k̂ − 4î;5}, so B⃗ = {10;c} × {3k̂ − 4î;5} = {1;c}(6k̂ − 8î) cos[6x + 8z − 10ct]."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 11 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"An electromagnetic wave of intensity 50 W m⁻² enters in a medium of refractive index ‘n’ without any loss. The ratio of the magnitudes of electric fields, and the ratio of the magnitudes of magnetic fields of the wave before and after entering into the medium are respectively, given by","o":["(√n, {1;√n})","(√n, √n)","({1;√n}, {1;√n})","({1;√n}, √n)"],"c":0,"s":"With no loss the intensity is unchanged: ½ε₀cE₁² = ½ε₀(n²)(c/n)E₂², i.e. E₁² = nE₂², so E₁/E₂ = √n.\nAfter entering, B₂ = {E₂;v} = {nE₂;c}, and B₁ = {E₁;c}. So {B₁;B₂} = {E₁;nE₂} = {√n;n} = {1;√n}.\nThe ratios are (√n, 1/√n)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 11 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"A 27 mW laser beam has a cross-sectional area of 10 mm². The magnitude of the maximum electric field in this electromagnetic wave is given by [Given permittivity of space ε₀ = 9 × 10⁻¹² SI units, speed of light c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s]","o":["2 kV/m","0.7 kV/m","1 kV/m","1.4 kV/m"],"c":3,"s":"I = {P;A} = {27 × 10⁻³;10 × 10⁻⁶} = 2700 W/m².\nI = ½ε₀cE₀², so E₀ = √{2I;ε₀c} = √{5400;9 × 10⁻¹² × 3 × 10⁸} = √(2 × 10⁶) ≈ 1.4 × 10³ V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"A light wave is incident normally on a glass slab of refractive index 1.5. If 4% of light gets reflected and the amplitude of the electric field of the incident light is 30 V m⁻¹, then the amplitude of the electric field for the wave propagating in the glass medium will be","o":["24 V m⁻¹","10 V m⁻¹","30 V m⁻¹","6 V m⁻¹"],"c":0,"s":"96% of the intensity is transmitted. In glass I = ½ε₀ncE_t² (v = c/n, ε = n²ε₀), so ½ε₀ncE_t² = 0.96 × ½ε₀cE_i².\nE_t² = {0.96 × 900;1.5} = 576, so E_t = 24 V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"The mean intensity of radiation on the surface of the Sun is about 10⁸ W/m². The rms value of the corresponding magnetic field is closest to","o":["10⁻² T","1 T","10⁻⁴ T","10² T"],"c":2,"s":"I = cε₀E_rms² = {c B_rms²;μ₀}, so B_rms = √{μ₀I;c} = √{4π × 10⁻⁷ × 10⁸;3 × 10⁸} = √(4.2 × 10⁻⁷) ≈ 6.5 × 10⁻⁴ T, closest to 10⁻⁴ T."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"An EM wave from air enters a medium. The electric fields are E⃗₁ = E₀₁x̂ cos[2πν({z;c} − t)] in air and E⃗₂ = E₀₂x̂ cos[k(2z − ct)] in medium, where the wave number k and frequency ν refer to their values in air. The medium is non-magnetic. If ε_r₁ and ε_r₂ refer to relative permittivities of air and medium respectively, which of the following options is correct?","o":["{ε_r₁;ε_r₂} = 4","{ε_r₁;ε_r₂} = 2","{ε_r₁;ε_r₂} = {1;4}","{ε_r₁;ε_r₂} = {1;2}"],"c":2,"s":"In air the wave number is k = 2πν/c. In the medium the wave is cos(2kz − kct) = cos(2kz − ωt), where ω = kc = 2πν is unchanged.\nSo the wave number in the medium is 2k: the wavelength halves, so n = 2 (v = c/2).\nFor a non-magnetic medium n² = ε_r₂/ε_r₁ = 4, so {ε_r₁;ε_r₂} = {1;4}."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018 (Online)","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"A monochromatic beam of light has a frequency ν = {3;2π} × 10¹² Hz and is propagating along the direction {î + ĵ;√2}. It is polarized along the k̂ direction. The acceptable form for the magnetic field is","o":["{E₀;c}{î + ĵ + k̂;√3} cos[10⁴{î + ĵ;√2}·r⃗ + (3 × 10¹²)t]","{E₀;c}{î − ĵ;√2} cos[10⁴{î + ĵ;√2}·r⃗ − (3 × 10¹²)t]","{E₀;c}{î − ĵ;√2} cos[10⁴{î − ĵ;√2}·r⃗ − (3 × 10¹²)t]","{E₀;c} k̂ cos[10⁴{î + ĵ;√2}·r⃗ + (3 × 10¹²)t]"],"c":1,"s":"ω = 2πν = 3 × 10¹² rad/s, so k = ω/c = 10⁴ m⁻¹.\nThe wave travels along n̂ = (î + ĵ)/√2, so the phase is k n̂·r⃗ − ωt (a minus sign); this rules out (a) and (d).\nB̂ = n̂ × Ê = (î + ĵ)/√2 × k̂ = (î − ĵ)/√2, perpendicular to both E (z) and n̂. Only (b) has this direction with the right phase ((c) has the wave vector along (î − ĵ), which is not the travel direction)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018 (Online)","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"A plane polarized monochromatic EM wave is travelling in vacuum along z direction such that at t = t₁ it is found that the electric field is zero at a spatial point z₁. The next zero that occurs in its neighbourhood is at z₂. The frequency of the electromagnetic wave is","o":["{1;t₁ + {|z₂ − z₁|;3 × 10⁸}}","{3 × 10⁸;|z₂ − z₁|}","{6 × 10⁸;|z₂ − z₁|}","{1.5 × 10⁸;|z₂ − z₁|}"],"c":3,"s":"At a fixed time E ∝ sin(kz − ωt) is zero at points half a wavelength apart: |z₂ − z₁| = λ/2, so λ = 2|z₂ − z₁|.\nf = {c;λ} = {3 × 10⁸;2|z₂ − z₁|} = {1.5 × 10⁸;|z₂ − z₁|}."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018 (Online)","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave of wavelength λ has an intensity I. It is propagating along the positive Y-direction. The allowed expressions for the electric and magnetic fields are given by","o":["E⃗ = √{I;ε₀c} cos[{2π;λ}(y − ct)] î; B⃗ = {1;c}E k̂","E⃗ = √{2I;ε₀c} cos[{2π;λ}(y + ct)] k̂; B⃗ = {1;c}E î","E⃗ = √{2I;ε₀c} cos[{2π;λ}(y − ct)] k̂; B⃗ = {1;c}E î","E⃗ = √{I;ε₀c} cos[{2π;λ}(y − ct)] k̂; B⃗ = {1;c}E î"],"c":2,"s":"I = ½ε₀cE₀², so E₀ = √{2I;ε₀c}: only (b) and (c) have this amplitude.\nThe wave travels along +y, so the phase is (y − ct): (b) with (y + ct) is out.\nCheck the directions in (c): E along k̂, B along î, and k̂ × î = ĵ, the +y direction."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2017 (Online)","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"Magnetic field in a plane electromagnetic wave is given by B⃗ = B₀ sin(kx + ωt) ĵ T\nExpression for corresponding electric field will be (where c is speed of light.)","o":["E⃗ = −B₀c sin(kx + ωt) k̂ V/m","E⃗ = B₀c sin(kx − ωt) k̂ V/m","E⃗ = {B₀;c} sin(kx + ωt) k̂ V/m","E⃗ = B₀c sin(kx + ωt) k̂ V/m"],"c":3,"s":"E₀ = cB₀, and E is in phase with B, with the same argument (kx + ωt): this leaves (a) and (d).\nThe argument kx + ωt means the wave travels along −x. B is along ĵ, and k̂ × ĵ = −î, so E must be along +k̂.\nE⃗ = B₀c sin(kx + ωt) k̂ V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2017 (Online)","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric field component of a monochromatic radiation is given by E⃗ = 2E₀î cos kz cos ωt.\nIts magnetic field is then given by","o":["{2E₀;c}ĵ cos kz cos ωt","{2E₀;c}ĵ sin kz cos ωt","{2E₀;c}ĵ sin kz sin ωt","−{2E₀;c}ĵ sin kz sin ωt"],"c":2,"s":"This is a standing wave. Use ∇ × E⃗ = −∂B⃗/∂t: (∇ × E⃗)_y = ∂E_x/∂z = −2E₀k sin kz cos ωt.\nSo ∂B_y/∂t = 2E₀k sin kz cos ωt, giving B_y = {2E₀k;ω} sin kz sin ωt = {2E₀;c} sin kz sin ωt."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2016 (Online)","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"Consider an electromagnetic wave propagating in vacuum. Choose the correct statement.","o":["For an electromagnetic wave propagating in +y direction the electric field is E⃗ = {1;√2}E_yz(x, t)ẑ and the magnetic field is B⃗ = {1;√2}B_z(x, t)ŷ.","For an electromagnetic wave propagating in +y direction the electric field is E⃗ = {1;√2}E_yz(x, t)ŷ and the magnetic field is B⃗ = {1;√2}B_yz(x, t)ẑ.","For an electromagnetic wave propagating in +x direction the electric field is E⃗ = {1;√2}E_yz(y, z, t)(ŷ + ẑ) and the magnetic field is B⃗ = {1;√2}B_yz(y, z, t)(ŷ + ẑ).","For an electromagnetic wave propagating in +x direction the electric field is E⃗ = {1;√2}E_yz(x, t)(ŷ − ẑ) and the magnetic field is B⃗ = {1;√2}B_yz(x, t)(ŷ + ẑ)."],"c":3,"s":"A plane wave must be transverse (E and B perpendicular to the travel direction), with E ⊥ B and E⃗ × B⃗ along the travel direction, and the fields depend only on the coordinate along the travel direction.\n(a), (b): the wave is along y, but the fields depend on x, and in (b) E is along the travel direction.\n(c): E and B are parallel, and the fields depend on y, z.\n(d): the fields depend on x, E ∝ (ŷ − ẑ) and B ∝ (ŷ + ẑ) are perpendicular, and (ŷ − ẑ) × (ŷ + ẑ) = 2x̂, along +x. So (d) is correct."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2015","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"A red LED emits light at 0.1 watt uniformly around it. The amplitude of the electric field of the light at a distance of 1 m from the diode is","o":["5.48 V/m","7.75 V/m","1.73 V/m","2.45 V/m"],"c":3,"s":"I = {P;4πr²} = {0.1;4π} ≈ 7.96 × 10⁻³ W/m².\nI = ½ε₀cE₀², so E₀ = √{2I;ε₀c} = √{0.0159;8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 3 × 10⁸} ≈ 2.45 V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2015 (Online)","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"For plane electromagnetic waves propagating in the z direction, which one of the following combination gives the correct possible direction for E⃗ and B⃗ field respectively?","o":["(î + 2ĵ) and (2î − ĵ)","(−2î − 3ĵ) and (3î − 2ĵ)","(2î + 3ĵ) and (î + 2ĵ)","(3î + 4ĵ) and (4î − 3ĵ)"],"c":1,"s":"Both fields must lie in the xy plane, be perpendicular to each other, and E⃗ × B⃗ must be along +ẑ.\n(c): dot product 2 + 6 ≠ 0, not perpendicular.\n(a): z-component of E × B = (1)(−1) − (2)(2) = −5, along −z. (d): (3)(−3) − (4)(4) = −25, along −z.\n(b): dot product (−2)(3) + (−3)(−2) = 0, and (−2)(−2) − (−3)(3) = +13, along +z. So (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2014","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"During the propagation of electromagnetic waves in a medium","o":["both electric and magnetic energy densities are zero","electric energy density is double of the magnetic energy density","electric energy density is half of the magnetic energy density","electric energy density is equal to the magnetic energy density."],"c":3,"s":"The electric and magnetic energy densities of an EM wave are equal at every point and instant (½εE² = B²/2μ, because E = vB)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2014 (Online)","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"An electromagnetic wave of frequency 1 × 10¹⁴ Hertz is propagating along z-axis. The amplitude of electric field is 4 V/m. If ε₀ = 8.8 × 10⁻¹² C²/N-m², then average energy density of electric field will be:","o":["35.2 × 10⁻¹⁰ J/m³","35.2 × 10⁻¹¹ J/m³","35.2 × 10⁻¹² J/m³","35.2 × 10⁻¹³ J/m³"],"c":2,"s":"The average of E² is E₀²/2, so the average electric energy density is ½ε₀ × {E₀²;2} = ¼ε₀E₀².\n= ¼ × 8.8 × 10⁻¹² × 16 = 35.2 × 10⁻¹² J/m³."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2013 (Online)","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave in a non-magnetic dielectric medium is given by E⃗ = E⃗₀(4 × 10⁻⁷x − 50t) with distance being in meter and time in seconds. The dielectric constant of the medium is","o":["2.4","5.8","8.2","4.8"],"c":1,"s":"The wave is a function of (4 × 10⁻⁷x − 50t) = 4 × 10⁻⁷(x − vt) with v = {50;4 × 10⁻⁷} = 1.25 × 10⁸ m/s.\nn = {c;v} = {3 × 10⁸;1.25 × 10⁸} = 2.4, and for a non-magnetic medium the dielectric constant is n² = 5.76 ≈ 5.8."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2013","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"The magnetic field in a travelling electromagnetic wave has a peak value of 20 nT. The peak value of electric field strength is","o":["12 V/m","3 V/m","6 V/m","9 V/m"],"c":2,"s":"E₀ = cB₀ = 3 × 10⁸ × 20 × 10⁻⁹ = 6 V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2013","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"A pulse of light of duration 100 ns is absorbed completely by a small object initially at rest. Power of the pulse is 30 mW and the speed of light is 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹. The final momentum of the object is","o":["0.3 × 10⁻¹⁷ kg ms⁻¹","1.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ kg ms⁻¹","3.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ kg ms⁻¹","9.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ kg ms⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"Energy of the pulse U = Pt = 30 × 10⁻³ × 100 × 10⁻⁹ = 3 × 10⁻⁹ J.\nComplete absorption: p = {U;c} = {3 × 10⁻⁹;3 × 10⁸} = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ kg m/s."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2012","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"An electromagnetic wave in vacuum has the electric and magnetic fields E⃗ and B⃗, which are always perpendicular to each other. The direction of polarization is given by X⃗ and that of wave propagation by k⃗. Then","o":["X⃗ ∥ E⃗ and k⃗ ∥ E⃗ × B⃗","X⃗ ∥ B⃗ and k⃗ ∥ E⃗ × B⃗","X⃗ ∥ E⃗ and k⃗ ∥ B⃗ × E⃗","X⃗ ∥ B⃗ and k⃗ ∥ B⃗ × E⃗"],"c":0,"s":"The direction of polarization of a light wave is defined as the direction of its electric field, so X⃗ ∥ E⃗.\nThe wave travels along E⃗ × B⃗ (the Poynting vector), so k⃗ ∥ E⃗ × B⃗."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2006","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"The rms value of the electric field of the light coming from the sun is 720 N/C. The average total energy density of the electromagnetic wave is","o":["3.3 × 10⁻³ J/m³","4.58 × 10⁻⁶ J/m³","6.37 × 10⁻⁹ J/m³","81.35 × 10⁻¹² J/m³"],"c":1,"s":"The average total energy density is u = ε₀E_rms² (electric and magnetic parts are equal, each ½ε₀E_rms²).\nu = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × (720)² = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 5.18 × 10⁵ ≈ 4.58 × 10⁻⁶ J/m³."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2004","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"An electromagnetic wave of frequency υ = 3.0 MHz passes from vacuum into a dielectric medium with permittivity ε = 4.0. Then","o":["wavelength is doubled and the frequency remains unchanged","wavelength is doubled and frequency becomes half","wavelength is halved and the frequency remains unchanged","wavelength and frequency both remain unchanged."],"c":2,"s":"The frequency is fixed by the source and does not change when the wave enters a medium.\nWith ε = 4ε₀ (and μ = μ₀), n = √(εᵣ) = 2, so v = c/2, and λ = v/f is halved."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2004","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"A radiation of energy E falls normally on a perfectly reflecting surface. The momentum transferred to the surface is","o":["E/c","2E/c","Ec","E/c²"],"c":1,"s":"The radiation of energy E carries momentum E/c towards the surface. A perfect reflector sends it back, so the momentum changes from +E/c to −E/c and the surface gets 2E/c."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2002","tp":"nature","st":"Nature and production","q":"Electromagnetic waves are transverse in nature is evident by","o":["polarization","interference","reflection","diffraction"],"c":0,"s":"Only transverse waves can be polarized. Interference, reflection and diffraction occur for longitudinal waves too, so they do not show the transverse nature."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2023","tp":"nature","st":"Directions of E, B and the wave","q":"The electric field associated with an electromagnetic wave propagating in a dielectric medium is given by\nE⃗ = 30(2x̂ + ŷ) sin[2π(5 × 10¹⁴t − {10⁷;3}z)] V m⁻¹.\nWhich of the following option(s) is(are) correct?\n[Given : The speed of light in vacuum, c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹]","o":["B_x = −2 × 10⁻⁷ sin[2π(5 × 10¹⁴t − {10⁷;3}z)] Wb m⁻²","B_y = 2 × 10⁻⁷ sin[2π(5 × 10¹⁴t − {10⁷;3}z)] Wb m⁻²","The wave is polarized in the xy-plane with polarization angle 30° with respect to the x-axis.","The refractive index of the medium is 2."],"c":[0,3],"s":"The wave speed is v = {ω;k} = {2π × 5 × 10¹⁴;2π × 10⁷/3} = 1.5 × 10⁸ m/s, so n = c/v = 2: (d) is correct.\nThe wave travels along +z, so B⃗ = {1;v}(ẑ × E⃗). ẑ × x̂ = ŷ and ẑ × ŷ = −x̂, so ẑ × E⃗ = 30(2ŷ − x̂) sin[…].\nB_x = −{30;1.5 × 10⁸} = −2 × 10⁻⁷: (a) is correct. B_y = +4 × 10⁻⁷, so (b) is wrong.\nThe polarization angle with the x-axis has tan θ = 30/60 = 0.5, so θ ≈ 26.6°, not 30°: (c) is wrong."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 4 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"If an optical medium possesses a relative permeability of {10;π} and relative permittivity of {1;0.0885}, then the velocity of light is greater in vacuum than that in this medium by _____ times.\n(μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ H/m, ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² F/m, c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s)","n":6,"s":"c/v = n = √(μᵣεᵣ) = √({10;π} × {1;0.0885}) = √{10;0.278} = √35.97 ≈ 6.\nSo light is faster in vacuum by 6 times."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 22 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"disp","st":"Displacement current in a capacitor","q":"A parallel plate capacitor of area A = 16 cm² and separation between the plates 10 cm, is charged by a DC current. Consider a hypothetical plane surface of area A₀ = 3.2 cm² inside the capacitor and parallel to the plates. At an instant, the current through the circuit is 6A. At the same instant the displacement current through A₀ is _____ mA.","n":1200,"s":"The displacement current through the whole plate area equals the circuit current, 6 A, and is spread uniformly over the area A.\nThrough A₀: I_d = 6 × {A₀;A} = 6 × {3.2;16} = 1.2 A = 1200 mA."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 23 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"disp","st":"Displacement current in a capacitor","q":"A time varying potential difference is applied between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor of capacitance 2.5 μF. The dielectric constant of the medium between the capacitor plates is 1. It produces an instantaneous displacement current of 0.25 mA in the intervening space between the capacitor plates, the magnitude of the rate of change of the potential difference will be _____ Vs⁻¹.","n":100,"s":"The displacement current in the gap equals the conduction current: I_d = C{dV;dt}.\n{dV;dt} = {I_d;C} = {0.25 × 10⁻³;2.5 × 10⁻⁶} = 100 V/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"A point source of light is placed at the centre of curvature of a hemispherical surface. The source emits a power of 24 W. The radius of curvature of hemisphere is 10 cm and the inner surface is completely reflecting. The force on the hemisphere due to the light falling on it is _____ × 10⁻⁸ N.","n":4,"s":"Take a ray at angle θ to the axis. It hits the surface normally and returns along itself, so the force it exerts is 2dP/c along the ray; only the component 2dP cos θ/c along the axis survives by symmetry.\nPower per solid angle is P/4π, so F = {2;c} × {P;4π} ∫cos θ dΩ over the hemisphere = {2;c} × {P;4π} × π = {P;2c}.\nF = {24;2 × 3 × 10⁸} = 4 × 10⁻⁸ N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 31 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"In a medium the speed of light wave decreases to 0.2 times to its speed in free space. The ratio of relative permittivity to the refractive index of the medium is x : 1. The value of x is _____.\n(Given speed of light in free space = 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹ and for the given medium μᵣ = 1).","n":5,"s":"n = {c;v} = {1;0.2} = 5. With μᵣ = 1, εᵣ = n² = 25.\nεᵣ : n = 25 : 5 = 5 : 1, so x = 5."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"Nearly 10% of the power of a 110 W light bulb is converted to visible radiation. The change in average intensities of visible radiation, at a distance of 1 m from the bulb to a distance of 5 m will be a × 10⁻² W/m². The value of ‘a’ will be _____.","n":84,"s":"Visible power = 11 W. Intensity I = {P;4πr²}.\nAt 1 m: {11;4π} = 0.875 W/m². At 5 m: {11;100π} = 0.035 W/m².\nChange = 0.875 − 0.035 = 0.84 W/m² = 84 × 10⁻² W/m²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"The intensity of the light from a bulb incident on a surface is 0.22 W/m². The amplitude of the magnetic field in this light-wave is _____ × 10⁻⁹ T.\n(Given: Permittivity of vacuum ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C² N⁻¹m⁻², speed of light in vacuum c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","n":43,"s":"I = ½ε₀cE₀², so E₀ = √{2I;ε₀c} = √{0.44;8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 3 × 10⁸} ≈ 12.9 V/m.\nB₀ = {E₀;c} = {12.9;3 × 10⁸} ≈ 4.3 × 10⁻⁸ T = 43 × 10⁻⁹ T."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"disp","st":"Displacement current in a capacitor","q":"The displacement current of 4.425 μA is developed in the space between the plates of parallel plate capacitor when voltage is changing at a rate of 10⁶ V s⁻¹. The area of each plate of the capacitor is 40 cm². The distance between each plate of the capacitor is x × 10⁻³ m. The value of x is _____.\n(Permittivity of free space, ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C² N⁻¹ m⁻²)","n":8,"s":"I_d = C{dV;dt} with C = {ε₀A;d}.\nd = {ε₀A;I_d}{dV;dt} = {8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 40 × 10⁻⁴ × 10⁶;4.425 × 10⁻⁶} = 8 × 10⁻³ m, so x = 8."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"The electric field in a plane electromagnetic wave is given by\nE⃗ = 200 cos[(0.5 × 10³ {1;m})x − (1.5 × 10¹¹ {rad;s})t] ĵ {V;m}\nIf this wave falls normally on a perfectly reflecting surface having an area of 100 cm². If the radiation pressure exerted by the wave on the surface during a 10 minute exposure is {x;10⁹} {N;m²}. Find the value of x.","n":354,"s":"For a perfect reflector the pressure is p = {2I;c} with I = ½ε₀cE₀², so p = ε₀E₀².\np = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × (200)² = 3.54 × 10⁻⁷ N/m² = {354;10⁹} N/m² (taking ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹²). The area and exposure time do not matter for the pressure.\nx = 354."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Wave equation: find E₀, B₀, λ, f, k, ω","q":"A plane electromagnetic wave with frequency of 30 MHz travels in free space. At particular point in space and time, electric field is 6 V/m. The magnetic field at this point will be x × 10⁻⁸ T. The value of x is _____.","n":2,"s":"B = {E;c} = {6;3 × 10⁸} = 2 × 10⁻⁸ T, so x = 2."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 31 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"The electric field in an electromagnetic wave is given by E = (50 N C⁻¹) sinω(t − {x;c}). The energy contained in a cylinder of volume V is 5.5 × 10⁻¹² J. The value of V is _____ cm³.\n(Given : ε₀ = 8.8 × 10⁻¹² C²N⁻¹m⁻²)","n":500,"s":"Average energy density (electric + magnetic) u = ½ε₀E₀² = ½ × 8.8 × 10⁻¹² × 2500 = 1.1 × 10⁻⁸ J/m³.\nV = {5.5 × 10⁻¹²;1.1 × 10⁻⁸} = 5 × 10⁻⁴ m³ = 500 cm³."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 17 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Radiation pressure and force","q":"If 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ N average force is exerted by a light wave on a non-reflecting surface of 30 cm² area during 40 minutes of time span, the energy flux of light just before it falls on the surface is _____ W/cm².\n(Round off to the nearest integer)\n(Assume complete absorption and normal incidence conditions are there)","n":25,"s":"For complete absorption F = {IA;c}, so I = {Fc;A} = {2.5 × 10⁻⁶ × 3 × 10⁸;30 × 10⁻⁴} = 2.5 × 10⁵ W/m² = 25 W/cm². The 40 minutes is not needed."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 17 Mar · Shift 2","tp":"disp","st":"Displacement current in a capacitor","q":"Sea water at a frequency f = 9 × 10² Hz, has permittivity ε = 80ε₀ and resistivity ρ = 0.25 Ω m. Imagine a parallel plate capacitor is immersed in seawater and is driven by an alternating voltage source V(t) = V₀ sin(2πft). Then the conduction current density becomes 10ˣ times the displacement current density after time t = {1;800} s. The value of x is _____.\n(Given: {1;4πε₀} = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻²)","n":6,"s":"With E = E₀ sin ωt: conduction density J_c = {E;ρ} = {E₀ sin ωt;ρ}, displacement density J_d = ε{dE;dt} = εωE₀ cos ωt.\n{J_c;J_d} = {tan ωt;ρεω}. Here ωt = 2π × 900 × {1;800} = 2.25π, so tan ωt = tan 45° = 1.\nε = 80 × {1;4π × 9 × 10⁹} = 7.07 × 10⁻¹⁰ F/m and ω = 2π × 900 = 5655 rad/s, so ρεω = 0.25 × 7.07 × 10⁻¹⁰ × 5655 = 10⁻⁶.\nThe ratio is 10⁶, so x = 6."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 17 Mar · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"The electric field intensity produced by the radiation coming from a 100 W bulb at a distance of 3 m is E. The electric field intensity produced by the radiation coming from 60 W at the same distance is √{x;5} E, where the value of x = _____.","n":3,"s":"At a fixed distance I ∝ P and I ∝ E², so E ∝ √P.\nE′ = E√{60;100} = E√{3;5}, so x = 3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 24 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"An electromagnetic wave of frequency 5 GHz is travelling in a medium whose relative electric permittivity and relative magnetic permeability both are 2. Its velocity in this medium is _____ × 10⁷ m/s.","n":15,"s":"v = {c;√(μᵣεᵣ)} = {3 × 10⁸;√(2 × 2)} = 1.5 × 10⁸ m/s = 15 × 10⁷ m/s. The frequency is not needed."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 24 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"An electromagnetic wave of frequency 3 GHz enters a dielectric medium of relative electric permittivity 2.25 from vacuum. The wavelength of this wave in that medium will be _____ × 10⁻² cm.","n":667,"s":"n = √εᵣ = 1.5 (non-magnetic), and the frequency stays 3 GHz.\nλ = {c;nf} = {3 × 10⁸;1.5 × 3 × 10⁹} = 0.0667 m = 6.67 cm = 667 × 10⁻² cm."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"energy","st":"Intensity of a source and the field amplitude","q":"A radiation is emitted by 1000 W bulb and it generates an electric field and magnetic field at P, placed at a distance of 2 m. The efficiency of the bulb is 1.25%. The value of peak electric field at P is x × 10⁻¹ V/m. Value of x is _____. (Rounded off to the nearest integer)\n[Take ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C² N⁻¹ m⁻², c = 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹]","n":137,"s":"Radiated power = 1.25% of 1000 W = 12.5 W. At 2 m: I = {12.5;4π × 4} = 0.249 W/m².\nI = ½ε₀cE₀², so E₀ = √{2I;ε₀c} = √{0.497;8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 3 × 10⁸} ≈ 13.7 V/m = 137 × 10⁻¹ V/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 25 Jan · Shift 2 (also 29 Jan · Shift 1, 30 Jan · Shift 2)","tp":"disp","st":"Maxwell’s equations and the displacement term","q":"Match List I with List II\nList I:\n(A) ∮B⃗·dl⃗ = μ₀i_c + μ₀ε₀{dφ_E;dt}\n(B) ∮E⃗·dl⃗ = −{dφ_B;dt}\n(C) ∮E⃗·dA⃗ = {Q;ε₀}\n(D) ∮B⃗·dA⃗ = 0\nList II:\n(I) Gauss’ law for electricity\n(II) Gauss’ law for magnetism\n(III) Faraday’s law\n(IV) Ampere–Maxwell law\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below.","o":["(A) → II, (B) → III, (C) → I, (D) → IV","(A) → I, (B) → II, (C) → III, (D) → IV","(A) → IV, (B) → III, (C) → I, (D) → II","(A) → IV, (B) → I, (C) → III, (D) → II"],"c":2,"s":"(A) has the displacement term μ₀ε₀ dφ_E/dt: Ampère–Maxwell law (IV).\n(B) relates the induced E to −dφ_B/dt: Faraday’s law (III).\n(C) flux of E equals Q/ε₀: Gauss’ law for electricity (I).\n(D) flux of B is zero: Gauss’ law for magnetism (II)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"nature","st":"Nature and production","q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): Electromagnetic waves carry energy but not momentum.\nReason (R): Mass of a photon is zero.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],"c":1,"s":"(A) is false: EM waves carry momentum as well as energy (momentum = energy/c), which is why they exert radiation pressure.\n(R) is true: a photon has zero rest mass."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 31 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"energy","st":"Energy density and the electric and magnetic parts","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Electromagnetic waves carry energy as they travel through space and this energy is equally shared by the electric and magnetic fields.\nStatement II: When electromagnetic waves strike a surface, a pressure is exerted on the surface.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct.","Both statement I and statement II are correct.","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect.","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect."],"c":1,"s":"Statement I is true: the electric and magnetic energy densities are equal.\nStatement II is true: the waves carry momentum, so they exert radiation pressure on a surface that absorbs or reflects them."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Nature and production","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Electromagnetic waves are not deflected by electric and magnetic field.\nStatement II: The amplitude of electric field and the magnetic field in electromagnetic waves are related to each other as E₀ = √{μ₀;ε₀} B₀.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","o":["Both statement I and statement II are false.","Both statement I and statement II are true.","Statement I is true but statement II is false.","Statement I is false but statement II is true."],"c":2,"s":"Statement I is true: the waves carry no charge, so static fields do not deflect them.\nStatement II is false: E₀ = cB₀ = B₀/√(μ₀ε₀). The expression √(μ₀/ε₀)B₀ does not even have the dimension of E."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"nature","st":"Speed and wavelength in a medium","q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion (A) and other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): A time varying electric field is a source of changing magnetic field and vice-versa. Thus a disturbance in electric or magnetic field creates EM waves.\nReason (R): In a material medium, the EM wave travels with speed v = {1;√(μ₀ε₀)}.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","o":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],"c":2,"s":"(A) is true: a changing E produces a changing B and vice versa, so a disturbance of either field spreads as an EM wave.\n(R) is false: in a material medium v = 1/√(με) = c/√(μᵣεᵣ), which is less than c. The speed 1/√(μ₀ε₀) is that in vacuum."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 24 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Arrange the following in the ascending order of wavelength (λ):\n(A) Microwaves (λ₁)  (B) Ultraviolet rays (λ₂)\n(C) Infrared rays (λ₃)  (D) X-rays (λ₄)\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","o":["λ₄ < λ₃ < λ₂ < λ₁","λ₄ < λ₂ < λ₃ < λ₁","λ₃ < λ₄ < λ₂ < λ₁","λ₄ < λ₃ < λ₁ < λ₂"],"c":1,"s":"In order of increasing wavelength: X-rays < ultraviolet < infrared < microwaves.\nThat is λ₄ < λ₂ < λ₃ < λ₁."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 4 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Arrange the following in the ascending order of wavelength.\n(A) Gamma rays (λ₁)  (B) X-rays (λ₂)\n(C) Infrared waves (λ₃)  (D) Microwaves (λ₄)\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["λ₄ < λ₃ < λ₂ < λ₁","λ₄ < λ₃ < λ₁ < λ₂","λ₂ < λ₁ < λ₄ < λ₃","λ₁ < λ₂ < λ₃ < λ₄"],"c":3,"s":"Wavelength increases as: gamma rays < X-rays < infrared < microwaves.\nThat is λ₁ < λ₂ < λ₃ < λ₄."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"The waves emitted when a metal target is bombarded with high energy electrons are","o":["Infrared rays","Radio Waves","X-rays","Microwaves"],"c":2,"s":"When fast electrons strike a metal target and are suddenly stopped (and knock out inner-shell electrons), X-rays are produced."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Which is the correct ascending order of wavelengths?","o":["λ_visible < λ_X-ray < λ_gamma-ray < λ_microwave","λ_gamma-ray < λ_X-ray < λ_visible < λ_microwave","λ_X-ray < λ_gamma-ray < λ_visible < λ_microwave","λ_microwave < λ_visible < λ_gamma-ray < λ_X-ray"],"c":1,"s":"Wavelength increases from gamma rays (about 10⁻¹² m) to X-rays (10⁻¹⁰ m), visible light (about 5 × 10⁻⁷ m) and microwaves (about 10⁻³ m)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 2","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Red light differs from blue light as they have","o":["Different frequencies and different wavelengths","Different frequencies and same wavelengths","Same frequencies and different wavelengths","Same frequencies and same wavelengths"],"c":0,"s":"Red and blue light are different parts of the visible spectrum: red has a longer wavelength (about 700 nm) and a lower frequency than blue (about 450 nm). In the same medium both move at the same speed, so v = fλ makes them differ in both f and λ."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 4 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Choose the correct option relating wavelength of different parts of electromagnetic wave spectrum.","o":["λ_radio waves > λ_micro waves > λ_visible > λ_X-rays","λ_visible > λ_X-rays > λ_radio waves > λ_micro waves","λ_X-rays < λ_micro waves < λ_radio waves < λ_visible","λ_visible < λ_micro waves < λ_radio waves < λ_X-rays"],"c":0,"s":"In decreasing order of wavelength the spectrum runs: radio waves, microwaves, visible light, X-rays. Only (a) lists them in that order."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2016","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Arrange the following electromagnetic radiations per quantum in the order of increasing energy\nA : Blue light  B : Yellow light\nC : X-ray  D : Radiowave","o":["D, B, A, C","A, B, D, C","C, A, B, D","B, A, D, C"],"c":0,"s":"Energy per quantum is E = hf, which increases with frequency.\nOrder of frequency: radiowave < yellow light < blue light < X-ray, i.e. D, B, A, C."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2016 (Online)","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Microwave oven acts on the principle of","o":["giving rotational energy to water molecules","giving translational energy to water molecules","giving vibrational energy to water molecules","transferring electrons from lower to higher energy levels in water molecule."],"c":0,"s":"Water molecules are polar. Microwaves (about 2.45 GHz) match the energy of rotational levels of water molecules, which absorb it as rotational energy and heat the food."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2002","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Which of the following are not electromagnetic waves?","o":["cosmic rays","gamma rays","β-rays","X-rays"],"c":2,"s":"β-rays are streams of fast electrons (charged particles), not electromagnetic waves. Gamma rays and X-rays are EM waves."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2002","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Infrared radiation is detected by","o":["spectrometer","pyrometer","nanometer","photometer"],"c":1,"s":"A pyrometer detects and measures infrared (heat) radiation. A photometer measures visible light intensity; a nanometer is a unit of length."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (EM-wave):\n(A) Infra-red\n(B) Ultraviolet\n(C) X-rays\n(D) Gamma rays\nList-II (Wavelength Range):\n(I) < 10⁻³ nm\n(II) 400 nm to 1 nm\n(III) 1 mm to 700 nm\n(IV) 1 nm to 10⁻³ nm\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],"c":2,"s":"Infrared: 1 mm to 700 nm (III). Ultraviolet: 400 nm to 1 nm (II). X-rays: 1 nm to 10⁻³ nm (IV). Gamma rays: below 10⁻³ nm (I)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 15 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Match List-I with List-II of electromagnetic waves with corresponding wavelength range.\nList I:\n(A) Microwave\n(B) Ultraviolet\n(C) X-Ray\n(D) Infra-red\nList II:\n(I) 400 nm to 1 nm\n(II) 1 nm to 10⁻³ nm\n(III) 1 mm to 700 nm\n(IV) 0.1 m to 1 mm\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below.","o":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"],"c":1,"s":"Microwaves: 0.1 m to 1 mm (IV). Ultraviolet: 400 nm to 1 nm (I). X-rays: 1 nm to 10⁻³ nm (II). Infrared: 1 mm to 700 nm (III)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 1 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(A) Microwaves\n(B) Gamma rays\n(C) Radio waves\n(D) X-rays\nList II:\n(I) Radioactive decay of nucleus\n(II) Rapid acceleration and deceleration of electron in aerials\n(III) Inner shell electrons\n(IV) Klystron valve\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below.","o":["(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],"c":2,"s":"Microwaves come from a klystron valve (IV). Gamma rays come from radioactive decay of a nucleus (I). Radio waves come from rapidly accelerated and decelerated electrons in an aerial (II). X-rays come from inner-shell electrons (III)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 31 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(A) Microwaves\n(B) UV rays\n(C) Infra-red light\n(D) X-ray\nList II:\n(I) Physiotherapy\n(II) Treatment of cancer\n(III) Lasik eye surgery\n(IV) Aircraft navigation\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below.","o":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)"],"c":2,"s":"Microwaves are used in radar and aircraft navigation (IV). UV rays are used in Lasik eye surgery (III). Infrared light is used in physiotherapy (I). X-rays (and gamma rays) are used to treat cancer (II)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) UV rays\n(B) X-rays\n(C) Microwave\n(D) Infrared wave\nList-II:\n(i) Diagnostic tool in medicine\n(ii) Water purification\n(iii) Communication, Radar\n(iv) Improving visibility in foggy days\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iv)","(A)-(ii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iii), (D)-(iv)","(A)-(ii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(iii), (D)-(i)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(i), (C)-(ii), (D)-(iv)"],"c":1,"s":"UV rays kill germs, so they purify water (ii). X-rays are a diagnostic tool (i). Microwaves are used in communication and radar (iii). Infrared scatters less than visible light, so it improves visibility in fog (iv)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Match List-I with List-II\nList-I:\n(A) Ultraviolet rays\n(B) Microwaves\n(C) Infrared waves\n(D) X-rays\nList-II:\n(i) Study crystal structure\n(ii) Greenhouse effect\n(iii) Sterilizing surgical instrument\n(iv) Radar system\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below.","o":["(A)-(iii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(ii), (D)-(i)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(i), (C)-(ii), (D)-(iv)","(A)-(iv), (B)-(iii), (C)-(ii), (D)-(i)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(i), (D)-(ii)"],"c":0,"s":"UV rays sterilize surgical instruments (iii). Microwaves are used in radar (iv). Infrared waves are responsible for the greenhouse effect (ii). X-rays have wavelengths comparable to atomic spacing, so they are used to study crystal structure (i)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 5 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"The correct match between the entries in column I and column II are\nColumn I (Radiation):\n(A) Microwave\n(B) Gamma rays\n(C) A.M. radio waves\n(D) X-rays\nColumn II (Wavelength):\n(i) 100 m\n(ii) 10⁻¹⁵ m\n(iii) 10⁻¹⁰ m\n(iv) 10⁻³ m","o":["(A)-(ii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iv), (D)-(iii)","(A)-(i), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iv)","(A)-(iv), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iii)"],"c":3,"s":"Microwaves: about 10⁻³ m (iv). Gamma rays: about 10⁻¹⁵ m (ii). A.M. radio waves: hundreds of metres (i). X-rays: about 10⁻¹⁰ m (iii)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 24 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Source of microwave frequency\n(B) Source of infrared frequency\n(C) Source of Gamma Rays\n(D) Source of X-rays\nList-II:\n(i) Radioactive decay of nucleus\n(ii) Magnetron\n(iii) Inner shell electrons\n(iv) Vibration of atoms and molecules\n(v) LASER\n(vi) RC circuit\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below","o":["(A)-(vi), (B)-(v), (C)-(i), (D)-(iv)","(A)-(ii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(vi), (D)-(iii)","(A)-(ii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(i), (D)-(iii)","(A)-(vi), (B)-(iv), (C)-(i), (D)-(v)"],"c":2,"s":"Microwaves come from a magnetron (ii). Infrared comes from vibrations of atoms and molecules (iv). Gamma rays come from radioactive decay of a nucleus (i). X-rays come from transitions of inner-shell electrons (iii)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2014","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Match List-I (Electromagnetic wave type) with List-II (Its association/application) and select the correct option from the choices given below the lists.\nList-I:\n(A) Infrared waves\n(B) Radio waves\n(C) X-rays\n(D) Ultraviolet rays\nList-II:\n(i) To treat muscular strain\n(ii) For broadcasting\n(iii) To detect fracture of bones\n(iv) Absorbed by the ozone layer of the atmosphere\nOptions (A, B, C, D):","o":["(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)","(iv), (iii), (ii), (i)","(i), (ii), (iv), (iii)","(iii), (ii), (i), (iv)"],"c":0,"s":"Infrared waves are used to treat muscular strain (i). Radio waves are used for broadcasting (ii). X-rays detect fractures of bones (iii). Ultraviolet rays are absorbed by the ozone layer (iv)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 12 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"spec","st":"Parts of the electromagnetic spectrum","q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: EM waves used for optical communication have longer wavelengths than that of microwave, employed in Radar technology.\nReason R: Infrared EM waves are more energetic than microwaves (used in Radar).\nIn the light of given statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","o":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is false but R is true.","A is true but R is false."],"c":2,"s":"Optical communication uses infrared or visible light (about 10⁻⁶ m), whose wavelength is shorter than that of microwaves (about 10⁻³ m). So A is false.\nInfrared has a higher frequency than microwaves, so its photons have more energy (E = hf): R is true."},
];
